{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation","id":1354207,"title":"Thoughts on why science is stagnant in Creation","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-10T10:52:18-08:00","id":1354207,"title":"Thoughts on why science is stagnant in Creation","content":"The state of technology in Creation is fascinating. It's been over 700 years since the last major catastrophe, a catastrophe that didn't send human understanding of the world back to the Stone Age. It's a world with thousands of people with super-human capacity to invent and innovate - a Terrestrial Exalt with just a Lore and Craft Excellency is capable of feats of thought and craftsmanship so far beyond Einstein and Da Vinci as to make them look like college frat boys. Yet it's a world that hasn't really made it out of the Iron Age. Considering that the Black Plague killed ~60% of Europe's population back in the 1350s and today we've been to the Moon and are working on traveling to Mars, what's holding back Creation? Creation even has advantages that our real world doesn't have, like a longer growing season and more landmass to support large populations.<br /> <br /> A thought came to me while I was considering the futility of arguments for or against the existence of God: the scientific method doesn't work properly in Creation. Here in our world, we can reliably perform experiments. When we burn hydrogen and oxygen, we always get water. If we drop something, it always falls towards the center of mass. So on and so forth. We can design experiments that allow us to hold the majority of things constant and only adjust our experimental variables.<br /> <br /> In Creation, however, the world is animistic and it's difficult to control enough variables. You are doing Mendel Gregor's pea plant experiments, just about to work out dominant and recessive genes, and then along comes the god of pea plants, who likes you because of how many pea plants you've been growing, and then &quot;blam&quot; suddenly your short pea plant strain now produces children that are taller than your tall strain. Or you are trying to work out the equations for gravity, but the Loom of Fate chokes on a demon's influence in the next province over and so today feathers are falling slower than they should. The best you can get with a simple experiment is a &quot;most of the time, this seems to be the result&quot;. That's fine for most things, but the more complicated the thing you are trying to get to work, the more you are piling uncertainty upon uncertainty until the result is a complete roll of the dice.<br /> <br /> Perhaps the easiest way to grasp what this means in practical terms is that in Creation, anything you hear could be true. If the lady next door shows you a gold nugget and told you she pulled it out of her oven instead of the cornbread she had been trying to make, that could actually be the case. Baking another batch of cornbread in the exact same oven and getting a loaf of cornbread wouldn't disprove her claim. Her experience could be one of those one-offs, where a god decided to influence things or a sorcerous working went sideways somewhere or the pattern spiders decided her fate needed to change.<br /> <br /> Figured I'd share this and see what other people thought about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T11:10:05-08:00","id":1354212,"title":"","content":"There's a sidebar in The Realm talking about how technical knowledge has been developed and refined over the centuries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T11:22:43-08:00","id":1354214,"title":"","content":"Also consider how sufficiently powerful Sorcerers can alter the fabric of the cosmos.<br /> <br /> <i>Assistant : &quot;Professor, our measurements on catapult ballistics were accurate after all. It turns out the the Anathema Wings-of-Golden-Truth lowered the gravitational constant by 0.1 last week.&quot;<br /> <br /> Professor : &quot;What, again?!&quot;</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25285-epitome","name":"Epitome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25285_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T11:52:50-08:00","id":1354223,"title":"","content":"I think that sounds roughly correct Kyuendo. I tend to think that &quot;science&quot; is fine, in the sense of &quot;The scientific method still makes logical sense in this world, and they've thought of it&quot;, and the problem is more that they don't have many scientific instruments that can actually work on Essence, and replication is much harder (largely because of much larger variability in underlying laws and conditions, like you suggest), so the scope for discovering usable and true scientific objectivity / &quot;laws&quot; that can generate technological innovation is limited. Combined with not for some reason due to the construction of their world not having as much scope in using fossil fuels and electricity for energy in ways that allow them to avoid the issues that Essence has (some Exalt might be able to magic up a framework where they do, but that would probably be a local exception to rules that worked as long as the Essence-craft held out).<br /> <br /> Their actual ability to conduct science within this scope is fine, they just can't really do energy &amp; computing industrial revolutions with it like we can, and in practice depend more on experience based learning by extraordinary individuals than they do on scientific method.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T11:54:27-08:00","id":1354224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation#post1354207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The state of technology in Creation is fascinating. It's been over 700 years since the last major catastrophe, a catastrophe that didn't send human understanding of the world back to the Stone Age. It's a world with thousands of people with super-human capacity to invent and innovate - a Terrestrial Exalt with just a Lore and Craft Excellency is capable of feats of thought and craftsmanship so far beyond Einstein and Da Vinci as to make them look like college frat boys. Yet it's a world that hasn't really made it out of the Iron Age. Considering that the Black Plague killed ~60% of Europe's population back in the 1350s and today we've been to the Moon and are working on traveling to Mars, what's holding back Creation? Creation even has advantages that our real world doesn't have, like a longer growing season and more landmass to support large populations.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Contagion is closer to the Bronze Age Collapse economically, and the collapse of the Americas populaiton-wise. These kind of need to be thought of there. You also kind of stack the deck weirdly when you compare 1350s Europe to now. Creation, after the Contagion, was probably pulling barely after the Sea Peoples destroyed a lot of the Levant and Eastern Mediterrainean if that.<br /> <br /> This is also tied a bit I think to a false belief of &quot;technological teleology&quot; as I kind of htink on it. Just becaus you have a lot of smart people doesn't mean technology automagically progresses in some expected curve. Especially one that look slike the last seven centuries with industrializtion. Technological progress and innovation works at a pretty steady rate. The First Age was built on five millennia of build-up, which had a huge chunk torn down by Solars during the Usurpation, destroyed in the Shogunate, or further demolished in the Contagion. You then had folks fighting over to survive with what's left rather htan rebuild for a few centuries. Creation looked more like Mad Max (or the Mississippi river region about a century after Spanish contact) than it did England in teh 1400s. And note that a lot of the things that created the higher standard of living were built on various custom-made items, by crafters who could generate unique components, and supported by a globalized infrastructure. If you were dropped in teh 1300s, no matter how smart you are you're probably just never building a PC.<br /> <br /> Note population and food doesn't in itself mean much either. China and India had larger populations than Europe for pretty much...ever. Tenochitlan was the largest city in the world when the Spanish showed up. Incan potatos were more stable than anything the Europeans had as a crop. Again, just becuase there's the things to create &quot;progress&quot; doesn't mean it happens. Our particular world is a result of particular circumastances that culminated at right places and right times. The Industrial Revolution was as much, to me at least, dumb luck and economic circumstancs as anything. The Aztecs had toys with wheels on them. No one though to make pull-carts with wheels on them. This doesn't mean they're dumb or that they never had geniuss. Just that for a lot of recorded human history said potential genius was a farmer who lived their life never getting even the chance to put 1 and 2 together.<br /> <br /> This note, is even with Exalts. They can speed things up, but being good at stuff doens' tmean you have the idea to do that stuff. High traits don't magically mean you even ahve the idea to do something. There have always been Einsteins throughout history, just that only one managed to leanr calculus and have time to think on physics like ours did.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation#post1354207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A thought came to me while I was considering the futility of arguments for or against the existence of God: the scientific method doesn't work properly in Creation. Here in our world, we can reliably perform experiments. When we burn hydrogen and oxygen, we always get water. If we drop something, it always falls towards the center of mass. So on and so forth. We can design experiments that allow us to hold the majority of things constant and only adjust our experimental variables.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a particular way to view scientific theory, but works good enough for this. Just note that like...there's a lot more implied in this than typically is thought. Philosophers of science, physicists and biologists have a lto to say on things like htis like how much chaos effs up all predictability, and how much we can link observation with causation. And even then, how much scientific observationr esults in practical engineering applicaiton, which is what this is more about than I think scientific knoweldge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation#post1354207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In Creation, however, the world is animistic and it's difficult to control enough variables. You are doing Mendel Gregor's pea plant experiments, just about to work out dominant and recessive genes, and then along comes the god of pea plants, who likes you because of how many pea plants you've been growing, and then &quot;blam&quot; suddenly your short pea plant strain now produces children that are taller than your tall strain. Or you are trying to work out the equations for gravity, but the Loom of Fate chokes on a demon's influence in the next province over and so today feathers are falling slower than they should. The best you can get with a simple experiment is a &quot;most of the time, this seems to be the result&quot;. That's fine for most things, but the more complicated the thing you are trying to get to work, the more you are piling uncertainty upon uncertainty until the result is a complete roll of the dice.</div> </div> </div> </div>So Mendel is a funny case since it turns out that pea plants and fruit flies are actually among the few organisms which actually do exhibit such easy gene theory stuff. Part of why he was not as embraced until a few decades later was because a lot of stuff <i>doesn't</i> show that. Which goes to my previous bit. THere's probably been people as observant or curious as Mendel, who might have even done simialr experiments. He was lucky in being the mindset, means, and picking the right plant to do his research on.<br /> <br /> It also kind of is a thing that Creation looks like Earth enough superficially, but in order to implement some of the things that we can do on Earth with just enough engineering, you need magic in Creation to do. Take for instance chemistry. A lot of alchemy in Creation probably looks like Earth. You can make paper from wood pulp, shampoo from oil, or ink from charcoal and clay (been watching <i>Ascendance of a Bookworm</i>, shoot me). But some rules just aren't our rules. Fool's gold and saltpeter just aren't going to combine in the way that makes black powder, and firedust is just not that material either. A lot of things in Creation are the way they are in part becuase <i>that enforces the setting's narrative</i>. Creation is the technology level it is in part becuase that's what the setting wants to be for the stories it tells.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation#post1354207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps the easiest way to grasp what this means in practical terms is that in Creation, anything you hear could be true. If the lady next door shows you a gold nugget and told you she pulled it out of her oven instead of the cornbread she had been trying to make, that could actually be the case. Baking another batch of cornbread in the exact same oven and getting a loaf of cornbread wouldn't disprove her claim. Her experience could be one of those one-offs, where a god decided to influence things or a sorcerous working went sideways somewhere or the pattern spiders decided her fate needed to change.<br /> <br /> Figured I'd share this and see what other people thought about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>A few things to also remember is that again, a lot of the technological advance we've had in the alst three cneturies is kind of absurd. There have been smart, innovative people building on thigns for centuries. The steam engine kind of turbo-charged htins. There was a recently Minute Earth video which pointed out that while there i sthe CO2 hockey stick, there's pretty much a few dozen hockey sticks related to human development since 1800, such as life expectancy, disease survival, total population, total education, total literacy, total income, and so on. If you cut out the the industrial revolution, for most of human history since the advent of farming, life was very <i>consistent</i> truth be told. At least statistically. Whole series of books can be written about the history of a place the size of Englad afterall. But for a lot of people, tech innovations would happen, be mostly stuff the upper class used, and innovations would happen, but they would marginally increase things.<br /> <br /> Creation seven years after the Contagion is better off technologically than it was due to innovations, inventions, progress, and such since then. But remember that a lot of the world had to be made from scratch (the infrastructure of the Shogunate, expertise to run it, and ability to rebuild it was just outright gone). And it is possible that many of the technologies we take for granted as having perpetuated our hockey-stick of development (namely the steam engine and later on the turbine) can even be made in Creation without magic, which by its nature is relatively rare, owned by folks who ae powerful and can make there lives better with it without having to give shit to the general poplace, and usually not replicatable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T12:53:13-08:00","id":1354234,"title":"","content":"From what I've skimmed of The Realm, it sounds like there's a fairly good understanding of technology at the heart of the empire-- there are mentions of printing, telescopes, aqueducts, clockwork, and so on. It generally sounds a lot more Renaissance than bronze age (apart from gunpowder). I don't the the question is a matter of <i>science </i>so much as it is... everything else, really. It's the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Divergence\" target=\"_blank\">Great Divergence</a>-- why did western Europe push forward into colonialism and the industrial revolution, while similarly &quot;advanced&quot; societies in China, the Middle East, and elsewhere did not? <br /> <br /> And that... isn't really a question that's possible to answer. Looking at Wikipedia, there are theories involving everything from capitalism to geography to the random luck of &quot;happened to have population centers near coal deposits.&quot; But looking at some of them...<ul><li>You couldn't really have colonization in the same way as we had in the real world. The Threshold has always been there, there's no sudden shock of new wealth from the &quot;discovery&quot; of the Americas.</li> <li>The Realm has been a single stable entity for eight hundred years. No bloody religious schisms, no inter-kingdom ways, no competition, no <i>pressure. </i></li> <li>The Immaculate Order is pretty well entrenched, and was pretty much built from the ground up to keep things the same.</li> <li>Creation is <i>huge</i>. If you need more food, you can just plant more fields. There's less pressure to squeeze more out of a limited supply.</li> </ul><br /> And if all else fails, I guess blame the Sidereals?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12314-grod_the_giant","name":"Grod_the_giant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12314_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T13:31:54-08:00","id":1354241,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354224#post1354224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Contagion is closer to the Bronze Age Collapse economically, and the collapse of the Americas populaiton-wise. These kind of need to be thought of there. You also kind of stack the deck weirdly when you compare 1350s Europe to now. Creation, after the Contagion, was probably pulling barely after the Sea Peoples destroyed a lot of the Levant and Eastern Mediterrainean if that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe in terms of infrastructure, but not in terms of the ideas in people's heads. The farmers aren't going to forget about irrigation ditches and the blacksmith isn't going to forget how to work iron. When they go about rebuilding, they are going to rebuild largely the same way they lived as before, barring things that were foreign imports.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is also tied a bit I think to a false belief of &quot;technological teleology&quot; as I kind of htink on it. Just becaus you have a lot of smart people doesn't mean technology automagically progresses in some expected curve. Especially one that look slike the last seven centuries with industrializtion. </div> </div> </div>I don't think there's a necessary course to how technological progress must progress. Various advances that we've made are largely independent of each other, so the ordering of when we discovered how to harness steam wasn't really dependent upon the discovery of how to make lenses for microscopes and telescopes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Note population and food doesn't in itself mean much either. China and India had larger populations than Europe for pretty much...ever. </div> </div> </div>And China and India discovered plenty of things before Europe did. Shampoo, Gunpowder, the concept of zero, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Aztecs had toys with wheels on them. No one though to make pull-carts with wheels on them. This doesn't mean they're dumb or that they never had geniuss. </div> </div> </div>The use of the wheel by the Aztecs was limited more by the terrain and lack of domesticable animals than by the lack of the concept of wheeled vehicles. Alvarado in 1523 found that the defenders of the Quich'e capital had &quot;small portable castles&quot; on rollers that were pulled around by armed men on the battlefield, used to distribute munitions (I assume arrows, darts, and such) to warriors. The Spanish also reported, and were backed up by later acheological finds, that the Aztecs knew many of the same metalurgical techniques known to the Spaniards but used them mostly for decorative purposes because of the ready availability of obsidian for tool making.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This note, is even with Exalts. They can speed things up, but being good at stuff doens' tmean you have the idea to do that stuff. High traits don't magically mean you even ahve the idea to do something. There have always been Einsteins throughout history, just that only one managed to leanr calculus and have time to think on physics like ours did. </div> </div> </div>One of the things that high traits on your character sheet represent is the ability to come up with good ideas. You, the player, may not be able to solve a puzzle, but that is your inability limiting your character and why you are justified to ask the Storyteller &quot;Can I roll my Intelligence + Lore to have my character figure out the solution as she is probably smarter than me?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And even then, how much scientific observationr esults in practical engineering applicaiton, which is what this is more about than I think scientific knoweldge. </div> </div> </div>Here in the present? We find out plenty of academic information that we haven't yet made practical. The thing is that we often don't know when a particular thing is going to become practical in the future. Before 1979, computers spent 30 years as being a novelty or niche in application. Then VisiCalc came out and computers ended up a ubiquitous part of our world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So Mendel is a funny case since it turns out that pea plants and fruit flies are actually among the few organisms which actually do exhibit such easy gene theory stuff. Part of why he was not as embraced until a few decades later was because a lot of stuff <i>doesn't</i> show that. Which goes to my previous bit. THere's probably been people as observant or curious as Mendel, who might have even done simialr experiments. He was lucky in being the mindset, means, and picking the right plant to do his research on. </div> </div> </div>So what? The law of large numbers means that even if the chance of a particular thing happening is rare, the larger the number of attempts the more the probability of the thing happening approaches certainty. Every life lived is another roll of the dice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of things in Creation are the way they are in part becuase <i>that enforces the setting's narrative</i>. Creation is the technology level it is in part becuase that's what the setting wants to be for the stories it tells. </div> </div> </div>I realize that the setting is the way that the setting is so we can play the game that Exalted wants to be. I'm putting forward something that seems to be a good in-universe explanation for why it can function mostly like our real world and yet have hung technologically where it did.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A few things to also remember is that again, a lot of the technological advance we've had in the alst three cneturies is kind of absurd. There have been smart, innovative people building on thigns for centuries. The steam engine kind of turbo-charged htins. There was a recently Minute Earth video which pointed out that while there i sthe CO2 hockey stick, there's pretty much a few dozen hockey sticks related to human development since 1800, such as life expectancy, disease survival, total population, total education, total literacy, total income, and so on. If you cut out the the industrial revolution, for most of human history since the advent of farming, life was very <i>consistent</i> truth be told. At least statistically. Whole series of books can be written about the history of a place the size of Englad afterall. </div> </div> </div>Human thriving has always largely been based on &quot;How much work can I get done using something other than my own muscles?&quot;. Animal domestication, slavery, the waterwheel, the steam engine, internal combustion, electricity, etc. The Industrial Revolution was, after all, enabled by agricultural advancement that resulted in less manpower being needed to manage farms, leading to a huge pool of cheap unskilled labor in the form of displaced agricultural workers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T15:00:04-08:00","id":1354246,"title":"","content":"It's a Bronze Age setting. Between RY0 and RY768 technology has advanced just as much as between 2100BC and 1332BC.<br /> <br /> The books even make reference to new inventions like Cynis Mond's kites, or Haslanti crossbows and airships.<br /> <br /> Swords, axes, the pottery wheel, shampoo, firedust, the concept of zero have all been invented/reinvented all over Creation in the last 768 years. Go to Wavecrest in RY30 and they won't have those things. Go to Wavecrest in 768 and they will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T15:12:47-08:00","id":1354248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354224#post1354224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Note population and food doesn't in itself mean much either. China and India had larger populations than Europe for pretty much...ever.</div> </div> </div> </div>To go on a long tangent on this a little, there are apparently a bunch of complications to working this out:<br /> <br /> 1) China has a bunch of imperial census going back over a thousand years and those tend to provide similar and sometimes lower totals than estimates of European population, while modern estimates can provide much higher numbers... but it's difficult to seriously take some of these that estimate the imperial Chinese government was low counting but up a degree of up to 50-100% (the bureaucracy had it's limitations, but counting every one in two or two in three taxable heads?). Though modern estimates, even if they are overestimates relative to the census, still often have China only 20% up in total population compared to estimates for Europe (as a whole) for most of the series barring some exceptional periods like an apparent Song population boom (where the peak of the Southern Song could be something like twice as much).<br /> <br /> 2) For India there apparently aren't anything like the above, and more wild estimates until late Victorian censuses that give around 245 million in 1872. But again that's not much higher than Europe of the time and possibly lower.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't really endorse the whole theory of &quot;Two heads are better than one&quot; arguments where innovation across history is simply explained by population size, market size and social network size, but it is worth stating that the modern situation where China and India each both have something like twice the entire population of Europe (and elsewhere in Asia another amount equal again to Europe) is probably something very much of a historical aberration. Compared to one where each region was standing on more of an equal population size with Europe (while within each region the centre of population growth probably moves from more developed parts to less developed parts, e.g. population growth in Europe disproportionately centered on less sophisticated Northern and Eastern Europe compared to more sophisticated Italy, in China the South rather than the North, etc). It's very much to do with the divergences of the last two centuries. (This kind of means that some of the arguments that China and India were often un-innovative relative to their population size can easily be exaggerations. While a lot of innovation centered in Europe probably is explained by that being where quite a bit of global population was, particularly bits of the global population that had best access to innovations going back to the earliest dawn of civilization).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T15:23:38-08:00","id":1354252,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354246#post1354246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a Bronze Age setting. Between RY0 and RY768 technology has advanced just as much as between 2100BC and 1332BC.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exalted the game has never actually called itself bronze age. It actually has a lot of elements form solidly in the Classical Era of Rome and Persia, plus later things from Song China.<br /> <br /> It's if anything as Bronze Age as the Illiad is actually Bronze Age (ie, it's what Greeks writing things down later well into the Iron Age embelished about it). Or that Conan is prehistory but there's steel and crossbows. <i>That said</i>, it's moot a bit since your point on the time scale isn't bad to point out. Even assuming later dates, the difference between 700 BCE and CE 68, while notable, are not nearly as different as 2019 and 1351.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354246#post1354246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The books even make reference to new inventions like Cynis Mond's kites, or Haslanti crossbows and airships.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't be surprsied if part of the importance of Wu-Jian in the last century was folks figuring out some new sailing tricks iether.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354246#post1354246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Swords, axes, the pottery wheel, shampoo, firedust, the concept of zero have all been invented/reinvented all over Creation in the last 768 years. Go to Wavecrest in RY30 and they won't have those things. Go to Wavecrest in 768 and they will.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very much. Creation is actually outright called out having deveoped a lot and recovered in teh cneturies since the Contagion. It's just that it's more like you know, most of how human history develops, nto the last two and a hafl cneturies of super-hyper-fast development.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T16:11:23-08:00","id":1354258,"title":"","content":"For a more elaborate answer:<br /> <br /> First off, I don't buy into the idea that any of the Exalted are meaningfully more intelligent than anybody who has ever existed (helped by the fact that the game has never really portrayed anybody as being such). There are several reasons for that inclination on my part, among which are questions it raises such as &quot;how would you even portray that&quot; and &quot;doesn't that sound exhausting&quot;. Never mind that comparisons to Einstein are difficult when general relativity is not an observation that can be made in a universe that does not have planet or a vast expanse of vacuum in which they orbit stars in which to make queries about Newtonian motion or gravitation. Or that, while Leonardo of Vinci did stand out a bit in being somebody who independently possessed and pursued a notion that mechanical principles could be applied towards developing more sophisticated vehicles even to the point of flying, none of the designs he sketched are in the least bit practical, so in some respects it's not exactly hard to show him up. You need a bit more than an Excellency to make an effective flying machine, and the fact that it will work owes a tad less to you being much smarter than it does to the fact that there exists magic to facilitate the process.<br /> <br /> Second, I'm of a mind to assume that Creation contains savants (Exalted and mortal alike) who are much like real life people making technical and intellectual queries prior to the 19th century, i.e. individuals of means who can dedicate a lot of their time to making rigorous observations in a context where those don't exactly contribute to any grand transformation of the world (although they might contribute to a number of smaller transformation that are felt by the people benefitting from them while the structure of society remains the same). I can imagine that somebody in Creation who has dredged up and made functional the proper diagnostic tools is in a position to find the local equivalent of the Higgs Boson. What is done with that information? I imagine that the intellectual who found it regards it as very spiritually elevating, but for the moment that's about it.<br /> <br /> Having given more of a look, I'll give props to referring to the fact that farmers and blacksmiths have actually possessed and implemented fairly sophisticated information about aspects of the world, but I'll point out that these things were never found under circumstances resembling a modern technical rigour or systemic process of iterative development. It's a collection of ad hoc problem solving and improvisation, often systemized in the form of things like aphorisms or a kind of mystical terminology (frequently by people who were illiterate) and transmitted on a one to one basis. There are numerous flaws in such a circumstance, such as it having a very spotty capacity to innovate (although in fairness, innovations once discovered can then spread like wildfire as people integrate what they know works better), and being somewhat vulnerable to large knowledge gaps should the people teaching it die off. That said, why would there be an assumption that steel processing in Creation has not had periods of improvement over the centuries? For that matter, why not assume that at the time before the Contagion, they were widely employing methods reliant on still functional machinery that meant there was a lack of people learned or experienced in how to do that stuff by hand. If the real life apocalypse comes and all the smelting facilities are rendered non-functional, do you know where to find a blacksmith to provide services to you? I hope that person doesn't die off before things stabilize.<br /> <br /> I wonder if the fact that they've only recently developed the means to get ships on a commercial scale rather than the personal vessels of adventuring Exalts implies any developments in ship construction and navigation techniques and technology.<br /> <br /> Still, the main thing about all of this is commercial. Scientific inquiries exploded when social models developed and there was a monetary incentive towards providing for people making these inquiries and having a means to put them to profitable use. I'd say much of Creation lacks those models, or that stability. The Realm is not intellectually static, but it's still governed by a powerfully entrenched and conservative aristocracy. Much of the rest of the world is confined to smaller and rigidly controlled polities that don't afford much flexibility, or disrupted by various kinds of devils. Life can often be taken up by pursuits that leave little time for much investigation or development. The Guild might have potential to foster innovations, but I think that for the most part their society is currently inclined towards innovations in the form of finding ways to effectively exploit currently existing resources rather than much development of something new. <br /> <br /> There's a quality to the degree to which the world is dependent on engaging with the mythic and mystical in there as well sure, although I think I've been losing some of my taste for that lately. I'm at least inclined to not argue so heavily from that position. For now I'll point to all of those other driving factors.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T16:14:50-08:00","id":1354259,"title":"","content":"Exalted is \u201cBronze Age\u201d in that it evokes Iron Age stories set in the Bronze Age (like the Iliad). The actual tech and society in said stories tends to be closer to those of the tellers than the setting.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d ballpark the general tech level of the Realm, once you exclude the magic stuff, at first century Rome + China. Outwards from there you have an overall decline except for in approach to other infrastructure hubs like Prasad or the Haslanti.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T17:18:47-08:00","id":1354269,"title":"","content":"The tech in Creation is all over the place. The art for Ragara Feria suggests that she's just finished snapping shut a pocket watch and said &quot;Tighten up the fore topgallant Mister Winter, and load the long nines. The Empress may have her trade route yet.&quot; Sailing <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Past_-_1859_Her_Majesty%27s_Ship_Victoria_on_her_last_voyage_%28from_the_picture_by_W_Fred_Mitchell%29_RMG_PU6218.jpg/800px-Past_-_1859_Her_Majesty%27s_Ship_Victoria_on_her_last_voyage_%28from_the_picture_by_W_Fred_Mitchell%29_RMG_PU6218.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">something like this</a>. Although she also has a pretty distinct ito wrapped hilt for whatever sword she's using.<br /> <br /> On the other hand most armies in the world are using bronze weapons and armor, and slingers are still a popular combat unit. International trade is pretty gangbusters though. Probably it has something to do with just how stuff works in Creation. There's no gunpowder because there's no potassium nitrate. Oh there's saltpeter, that's for sure, but it's not KNO3, it's an ALchemical reagent, not a chemical one.<br /> <br /> <br /> The real answer is, of course, it has exactly enough technology to be cool and fun, without breaking the aesthetic. So they want really awesome age of sail fully rigged ships, but don't want everyone walking around with sidearm pistols strapped to their waists. Only some people sometimes, so it gets wonky.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T18:14:29-08:00","id":1354273,"title":"","content":"I have always been of the thought that the technological level of Creation is limited only by what was programmed into the Loom of Fate, one of whose functions is causality. If the Loom of Fate was programmed to not recognize the mixture of charcoal, sulfur and saltpeter as resulting in black powder then creation will not have black powder. Physics does not really exist in Creation, Physics is causality and causality is regulated by the Loom of Fate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800080\">I express my opinions, but YSMV; Your Stories May Vary.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:8px\"><i>Formerly known as wyrmpuff</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12288-mal-akh","name":"Mal'akh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12288_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T18:40:54-08:00","id":1354275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354241#post1354241\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe in terms of infrastructure, but not in terms of the ideas in people's heads. The farmers aren't going to forget about irrigation ditches and the blacksmith isn't going to forget how to work iron.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is, dead blacksmiths train no apprentices, and neither do blacksmiths that spend the last half of their life doing something else to survive. The Great Contagion killed 90% of <i>all living things</i> (core p.27), and <i>then</i> the Fair Folk invaded, and then the survivors of both of those tried to survive in the aftermath rather than just continuing what they were doing before. Who needs a blacksmith, anyway, when there are ten hoes laying around for every surviving farmer? Lands that need a labor force to maintain irrigation ditches are going to be abandoned now that the labor force is gone for now-90%-empty lands that have natural water. Lots of knowledge is going to be lost (except in surviving books) simply because there'll be a generation of disuse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T20:23:28-08:00","id":1354288,"title":"","content":"While I am firmly of the opinion that Creation is on the verge of an industrial revolution, it is worth noting that at least one force in Creation has been actively suppressing technological advancements across much of Creation. The Realm doesn't want Threshold states advancing technologically. It's much easier to maintain control of a population if their weapons and armor are centuries behind your own. They cant challenge you financially if they can't produce anything better than you. Keep the populations paying in raw materials rather than processed goods so that there isn't any incentive to advance manufacturing technology.<br /> <br /> They scavenger lands have not excuse though, they should be I a really good place technologically, especially with The Guild being based at their heart. The guild has every reason to drive advances in technology. More goods, better goods, cheaper goods, new goods, faster travel, better security, The Guild needs it all and is the reason Creation needs an Industrial Revolution.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T20:27:12-08:00","id":1354290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354275#post1354275\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The thing is, dead blacksmiths train no apprentices, and neither do blacksmiths that spend the last half of their life doing something else to survive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ghosts are a thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T21:27:15-08:00","id":1354299,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354290#post1354290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ghosts are a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, and they live in a realm of existence that only bleeds into Creation in a few select places. Entire swathes of communities can go their entire lives without encountering ghosts.<br /> <br /> \u200bAlso, considering the sort of shit you can smuggle into a work with Linguistics Charms, I can see why the Dragon-Blooded would have limited people's access to First Age manuals, erring on the side of caution, but tragically necessitating that people re-learn <i>everything</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 12-11-2019, 12:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T23:48:21-08:00","id":1354314,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354290#post1354290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ghosts are a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>And oh look, Skullstone is significantly more advanced than its neighbors. But that level of living/dead commingling and cooperation is an extreme outlier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-10T23:58:26-08:00","id":1354315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354288#post1354288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I am firmly of the opinion that Creation is on the verge of an industrial revolution, it is worth noting that at least one force in Creation has been actively suppressing technological advancements across much of Creation. The Realm doesn't want Threshold states advancing technologically. It's much easier to maintain control of a population if their weapons and armor are centuries behind your own. They cant challenge you financially if they can't produce anything better than you. Keep the populations paying in raw materials rather than processed goods so that there isn't any incentive to advance manufacturing technology.<br /> <br /> They scavenger lands have not excuse though, they should be I a really good place technologically, especially with The Guild being based at their heart. The guild has every reason to drive advances in technology. More goods, better goods, cheaper goods, new goods, faster travel, better security, The Guild needs it all and is the reason Creation needs an Industrial Revolution.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it might be that the Guild wants some places dirt poor and ass backwards. After all, slaves have to come from somewhere and the more pliant they are, presumably the better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8959-prototype00","name":"prototype00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8959_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"964"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T02:56:53-08:00","id":1354327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation#post1354207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The state of technology in Creation is fascinating. It's been over 700 years since the last major catastrophe, a catastrophe that didn't send human understanding of the world back to the Stone Age. It's a world with thousands of people with super-human capacity to invent and innovate - a Terrestrial Exalt with just a Lore and Craft Excellency is capable of feats of thought and craftsmanship so far beyond Einstein and Da Vinci as to make them look like college frat boys. Yet it's a world that hasn't really made it out of the Iron Age. Considering that the Black Plague killed ~60% of Europe's population back in the 1350s and today we've been to the Moon and are working on traveling to Mars, what's holding back Creation? Creation even has advantages that our real world doesn't have, like a longer growing season and more landmass to support large populations.<br /> <br /> A thought came to me while I was considering the futility of arguments for or against the existence of God: the scientific method doesn't work properly in Creation. Here in our world, we can reliably perform experiments. When we burn hydrogen and oxygen, we always get water. If we drop something, it always falls towards the center of mass. So on and so forth. We can design experiments that allow us to hold the majority of things constant and only adjust our experimental variables.<br /> <br /> In Creation, however, the world is animistic and it's difficult to control enough variables. You are doing Mendel Gregor's pea plant experiments, just about to work out dominant and recessive genes, and then along comes the god of pea plants, who likes you because of how many pea plants you've been growing, and then &quot;blam&quot; suddenly your short pea plant strain now produces children that are taller than your tall strain. Or you are trying to work out the equations for gravity, but the Loom of Fate chokes on a demon's influence in the next province over and so today feathers are falling slower than they should. The best you can get with a simple experiment is a &quot;most of the time, this seems to be the result&quot;. That's fine for most things, but the more complicated the thing you are trying to get to work, the more you are piling uncertainty upon uncertainty until the result is a complete roll of the dice.<br /> <br /> Perhaps the easiest way to grasp what this means in practical terms is that in Creation, anything you hear could be true. If the lady next door shows you a gold nugget and told you she pulled it out of her oven instead of the cornbread she had been trying to make, that could actually be the case. Baking another batch of cornbread in the exact same oven and getting a loaf of cornbread wouldn't disprove her claim. Her experience could be one of those one-offs, where a god decided to influence things or a sorcerous working went sideways somewhere or the pattern spiders decided her fate needed to change.<br /> <br /> Figured I'd share this and see what other people thought about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love your original post because this is what I try to explain over and over to a lot of players : Science doesn't mean anything in a lot of settings, because science depends on philosophical premises (like reconducing experiments in the same conditions, etc ...) and thus we can't have science in Exalted. I'd even go a bit further than you and say than even without the interference of nearby demons and gods in your neighborhood, the experiments would fail because, well, is the world even made of atoms and of the same physical laws that are universal in Creation ? I don't think so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T03:29:27-08:00","id":1354329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354327#post1354327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love your original post because this is what I try to explain over and over to a lot of players : Science doesn't mean anything in a lot of settings, because science depends on philosophical premises (like reconducing experiments in the same conditions, etc ...) and thus we can't have science in Exalted. I'd even go a bit further than you and say than even without the interference of nearby demons and gods in your neighborhood, the experiments would fail because, well, is the world even made of atoms and of the same physical laws that are universal in Creation ? I don't think so.</div> </div> </div> </div>While 2e heavily emphasized that everything in Creation is made of Essence on a fundamental level, in all editions there is an assumption that barring explicit magic interference, yes, things do work like in real world. There may be magical diseases cooked up by the Deathlords that make you crap out your soul, but cholera is still caused by bacteria in the water and avoidable with proper hygiene - and you could still cure it with antibiotics if you knew how.<br /> <br /> The problem with promoting the scientific method and worldview in those conditions is that once you encounter that Deathlord-brewed disease that makes you crap out your soul, all your knowledge of bacteria won't help you one bit, but that magical spring blessed by a goddess of healing still might. And if never drinking water that has been touched by fire is what's keeping the Fair Folk away from your tribe, you're pretty screwed if someone craps in your village well. So it's often easier to just place your trust in the supernatural.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T03:44:15-08:00","id":1354331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354329#post1354329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While 2e heavily emphasized that everything in Creation is made of Essence on a fundamental level, in all editions there is an assumption that barring explicit magic interference, yes, things do work like in real world. There may be magical diseases cooked up by the Deathlords that make you crap out your soul, but cholera is still caused by bacteria in the water and avoidable with proper hygiene - and you could still cure it with antibiotics if you knew how.<br /> <br /> The problem with promoting the scientific method and worldview in those conditions is that once you encounter that Deathlord-brewed disease that makes you crap out your soul, all your knowledge of bacteria won't help you one bit, but that magical spring blessed by a goddess of healing still might. And if never drinking water that has been touched by fire is what's keeping the Fair Folk away from your tribe, you're pretty screwed if someone craps in your village well. So it's often easier to just place your trust in the supernatural.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I'm fine with this interpretation (or this rule, if it's written explicitly), but I really prefer taking from the larger spectrum of &quot;We can't have science&quot; (or rather, some characters can have science because this is sort of their paradigm) where there is no clear objective statement about &quot;The world works like this&quot;, and it can then be left to the interpretation and powers of the characters to try to understand what is and what is not. A bit in the same way, I don't like playing in &quot;our world&quot; in a setting where the ST would say &quot;God objectively doesn't exist in my setting. Your religious character doesn't know it and he can be religious it's just fine, but God just doesn't exist&quot;. I feel this is sort of information that can take away a form of immersion from the player, even if this has obviously no effect on the character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T03:53:14-08:00","id":1354333,"title":"","content":"The rapid technological progress of the last century is an anomaly, history-wise. For a very long time new technologies were few and far between.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T06:17:37-08:00","id":1354342,"title":"","content":"Personally I think if we're keeping to &quot;90% of everything died&quot; Creation's recovery feels unrealistically fast compared to real life extinction level events.<br /> <br /> The Great Contagion needs to work like the Thanos snap to not eradicate important parts of the biosphere and drive a lot more creatures to extinction. This interpretation has also historically been pretty weird to me because the Deathlords actually wanted to kill everything not reduce it to more manageable levels.<br /> <br /> Also 768 years isn't really that long ago for 90% of the trees dying without serious magical assistance helping out with all that tree planting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T06:24:33-08:00","id":1354345,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354288#post1354288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They scavenger lands have not excuse though, they should be I a really good place technologically, especially with The Guild being based at their heart. The guild has every reason to drive advances in technology. More goods, better goods, cheaper goods, new goods, faster travel, better security, The Guild needs it all and is the reason Creation needs an Industrial Revolution.</div> </div> </div> </div>Scavenger Lands are littered with remnants of bygone ages that offer untold riches to those bold and smart enough to claim and use them. Why invest in alchemists to improve their technology of extracting cocaine from coca leaves when you can invest in an expedition to capture a dinosaur that pisses liquid cocaine? Why invest in your silver mining technology when a single artifact from a raided Solar tomb can net you a price that dwarfs the lifetime yield of several silver mines?<br /> <br /> And let's not forget slaves. I heard a theory recently that the Roman Empire didn't undergo an Industrial Revolution of its own because slave labor effectively obviated the need for automation. Dunno if that's true about Rome specifically, but it does stand to reason that cheap and abundant labor force will at least deprioritize technological advancements of the kind that led us to the Industrial Revolution.<br /> <br /> And if you can somehow combine magic and slave labor... now that's the kind of innovation the Guild really likes! Check out the story of Luna's Sorrow in <i>Masters of Jade</i> for a prime example of how the Guild innovates without bringing Creation anywhere closer to an industrial revolution like the one that happened in our world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T08:14:17-08:00","id":1354361,"title":"","content":"Necessity is the Mother of all invention.<br /> <br /> That's Creations problem.<br /> <br /> An exalt encountering a problem will probably solve it with magic (charms or sorcery). If that won't work he <i>could </i>produce a machine that solves his problem.<br /> <br /> But if a machine can solve his problem a <i>magical </i>machine will solve it <i>even better</i>. That machine could be reproduced by another exalt (perhaps a bit modified to better suit that exalts situation) but it won't become a technological breakthrough.<br /> <br /> So as far as exalts are concerned, they probably won't have a large technological impact. And the presence of exalted will slow down mortal invention overall. What's the easiest way for a mortal to solve a problem he can't solve on his own? Invent something noone ever thought of before? Or ask/pay/humbly request an exalt to solve his problem for him?<br /> <br /> I also has to agree that with an unreliable scientific principle and no fossil fuels something like our own technological revolution probably won't happen.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1226-gammelbraxen","name":"Gammelbraxen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T08:41:05-08:00","id":1354369,"title":"","content":"I don't feel like the supernatural elements of the setting are really present and active <i>enough</i> to be responsible for preventing the scientific method from taking root.<br /> <br /> Considering that the gods are a far cry from being all-powerful, that tampering in mortal affairs <i>is not</i> effortless, and gods tend to enjoy <i>not</i> having to exert themselves, I expect that oftentimes when a god or ghost <i>does</i> mess with someone doing something, it's probably going to be paired with a demand of some sort.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T08:52:46-08:00","id":1354372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354369#post1354369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't feel like the supernatural elements of the setting are really present and active <i>enough</i> to be responsible for preventing the scientific method from taking root.<br /> <br /> Considering that the gods are a far cry from being all-powerful, that tampering in mortal affairs <i>is not</i> effortless, and gods tend to enjoy <i>not</i> having to exert themselves, I expect that oftentimes when a god or ghost <i>does</i> mess with someone doing something, it's probably going to be paired with a demand of some sort.</div> </div> </div> </div>Culturally though there could still be a pushback against finding solutions that don't rely on the tradition of begging powerful beings for help. The ingenious mortal who invents a steam engine may very well be shunned as going against the natural order, and they may even be suspected of having achieved their engine by dealing with evil beings or by usurping the power of Holy Daana'd herself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25285,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25285-epitome","user":"Epitome","date":". 12-11-2019, 11:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25285-epitome","name":"Epitome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25285_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T08:55:55-08:00","id":1354376,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epitome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354372#post1354372\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Culturally though there could still be a pushback against finding solutions that don't rely on the tradition of begging powerful beings for help. The ingenious mortal who invents a steam engine may very well be shunned as going against the natural order, and they may even be suspected of having achieved their engine by cavorting with evil forces or by usurping the power of Holy Daana'd herself.</div> </div> </div> </div>I get that, and that's fair; I'm just saying the idea that the presence of the supernatural would somehow prevent the scientific method seems kinda silly, especially considering how many thousands of years humans existed in <b>our</b> world without it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T09:50:21-08:00","id":1354390,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354342#post1354342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I think if we're keeping to &amp;quot;90% of everything died&amp;quot; Creation's recovery feels unrealistically fast compared to real life extinction level events.<br /> <br /> The Great Contagion needs to work like the Thanos snap to not eradicate important parts of the biosphere and drive a lot more creatures to extinction. This interpretation has also historically been pretty weird to me because the Deathlords actually wanted to kill everything not reduce it to more manageable levels.<br /> <br /> Also 768 years isn't really that long ago for 90% of the trees dying without serious magical assistance helping out with all that tree planting.</div> </div> </div> </div>So Holden clarified that whilst the Great Contagion killed 90% of those it infected, it didn't infect 100% of Creation.<br /> <br /> 90% of Creation still died, however, due to biosphere collapse (and Fair Folk invasion).<br /> <br /> As for regrowth, Creation has the benefit of magic. It's recovered at supernatural speed, because it has supernatural magic speeding the recovery. This is not a perfect process. Stuff still gets lost. But Creation is probably more like Renaissance Europe than Bronze Age Europe in that respect -- it's about rediscovering the lost wisdom of the ancients and the distant wisdom of the land beyond the sea rather than innovation (but innovation like airboats and crossbows is still happening).<br /> <br /> Creation doesn't exist. But the setting makes perfect sense to me. It's exactly where I think it should be, and the established canon is comprehensive enough to draw justification from but too loose to disprove. I like it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T10:11:52-08:00","id":1354399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354342#post1354342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I think if we're keeping to &quot;90% of everything died&quot; Creation's recovery feels unrealistically fast compared to real life extinction level events.<br /> <br /> The Great Contagion needs to work like the Thanos snap to not eradicate important parts of the biosphere and drive a lot more creatures to extinction. This interpretation has also historically been pretty weird to me because the Deathlords actually wanted to kill everything not reduce it to more manageable levels.<br /> <br /> Also 768 years isn't really that long ago for 90% of the trees dying without serious magical assistance helping out with all that tree planting.</div> </div> </div> </div>At least in 3e it's only animal life, but it's still yeah, pretty effing catostrophic. I tend to assume that the schtizobioms Creation has with creatures form Earth over 250M year history was probably like...more somehow back before then. Which gets weird when you get shit like I dunno, trilobytes in the ocena or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T10:17:09-08:00","id":1354402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354376#post1354376\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get that, and that's fair; I'm just saying the idea that the presence of the supernatural would somehow prevent the scientific method seems kinda silly, especially considering how many thousands of years humans existed in <b>our</b> world without it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the big thing though to remember is that due to how the supernatural solves problems, it <i>won't</i> generally be the big driver for technological innovation that one might initialy think. The First Age is kind of a moot head start when after the Contagion you're basically at the tech level of, at best, China's Xia or Egypt's 19th Dynasties. Or in say, Greece, about as fucked as post-Minoan civilization. Or the Incan and just you know, eradicated with nothing like the Spanish to fill lin.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T10:39:11-08:00","id":1354411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354314#post1354314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And oh look, Skullstone is significantly more advanced than its neighbors. But that level of living/dead commingling and cooperation is an extreme outlier.</div> </div> </div> </div>Skullstone relies on corpses for cheap labor and salvage, though, and its dead are idle. An ancestor cult to preserve lost knowledge is something a bit different, and very demanding of blood (in past editions, summoning the dead was so easy that even those with Occult 0 could do it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-11T15:47:37-08:00","id":1354478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354390#post1354390\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for regrowth, Creation has the benefit of magic. It's recovered at supernatural speed, because it has supernatural magic speeding the recovery.</div> </div> </div> </div>Historically that's never been satisfying to me, because it says there was a major effort to repair Creation's biosphere that we've never heard anything about but have to assume happened despite it being somewhat counter to most of what we're told about Yu-Shan and the Elemental Dragons so ultimately it feels like &quot;a wizard did it&quot;.<br /> <br /> I'm sure 3rd edition will deliver a more satisfactory presentation when the time comes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":7,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","user":"Lioness","date":". 12-11-2019, 07:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T01:27:05-08:00","id":1354535,"title":"","content":"Something is more important in Creation's technological state than the understanding of scientific principles, I think, is just the fact that (and I'm willing to be contradicted here if something in the canon is unknown to me) there's no reason for fossil fuels to exist. Not by natural means, anyway. Dead things putrefying into oil or coal is a process that takes millions of years, time Creation just hasn't had (the time scale of Primordial rule doesn't seem like it was *that* long). Without fuel, technical cleverness has a very, very hard time scaling. There just isn't practicality in making, say, a steam engine if the requisite heat to make a number of them work can only come from cutting down and burning acre upon acre of forest. Even if some brilliant savant thought through the engineering of such a device, it would never be more than a curiosity. I suppose that fuel could be made magically, but in that case why not just skip the step and solve whatever problem the steam engine would have solved through sorcery directly?<br /> <br /> Ultimately, it seems to me that the only possible basis for major improvement in living standards at scale in Creation is magic, unless the gods or the Primordials antiqued Creation for some reason or put a bunch of fuel somewhere. Otherwise there's just not enough stored up energy to power an industrial society.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11685-sundance","name":"Sundance","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T01:37:34-08:00","id":1354536,"title":"","content":"Question. Is it possible to use elemental summoning and beckoning to power steam engines instead?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T01:55:48-08:00","id":1354538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354536#post1354536\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Question. Is it possible to use elemental summoning and beckoning to power steam engines instead?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see why not. Water boils the same way it does on Earth. If an elemental can heat water in a boiler to 100 degrees Celsius, it will turn to steam and create pressure.<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar177_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"177\" data-vbnamecard=\"177\">TheCountAlucard</a> aptly noted, supernatural beings in Creation are seldom capable of exerting any sort of supernatural power effortlessly. Unless you have an elemental that generates enough heat just by standing there. you'd need it to spend motes on heating the water. Which may or may not be more convenient than just keeping a mundane fire.<br /> <br /> Of course, <a href=\"https://www.smbc-comics.com/comic/magical-realism\" target=\"_blank\">the implications of all that are really grim</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T02:15:35-08:00","id":1354539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354478#post1354478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Historically that's never been satisfying to me, because it says there was a major effort to repair Creation's biosphere that we've never heard anything about but have to assume happened despite it being somewhat counter to most of what we're told about Yu-Shan and the Elemental Dragons so ultimately it feels like &amp;quot;a wizard did it&amp;quot;.<br /> <br /> I'm sure 3rd edition will deliver a more satisfactory presentation when the time comes.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's the Invisible Hand.<br /> <br /> The Dragons flood Creation with Essence. Not a conscious rebuilding effort: it just happens. Elementals (in 2E) form from Essence inbalance: no Yu Shen oversight, it just happens. Sidereals work tirelessly tugging Fate along, apocalypse or not. Local gods work little miracles in their domains all across Creation. Lunars and Dragon-Blooded actively try to rebuild.<br /> <br /> Each individual's great fight to just survive to the next day <b>is</b> the rebuilding effort.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T04:07:04-08:00","id":1354546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sundance</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354535#post1354535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something is more important in Creation's technological state than the understanding of scientific principles, I think, is just the fact that (and I'm willing to be contradicted here if something in the canon is unknown to me) there's no reason for fossil fuels to exist. Not by natural means, anyway. Dead things putrefying into oil or coal is a process that takes millions of years, time Creation just hasn't had (the time scale of Primordial rule doesn't seem like it was *that* long). Without fuel, technical cleverness has a very, very hard time scaling. There just isn't practicality in making, say, a steam engine if the requisite heat to make a number of them work can only come from cutting down and burning acre upon acre of forest. Even if some brilliant savant thought through the engineering of such a device, it would never be more than a curiosity. I suppose that fuel could be made magically, but in that case why not just skip the step and solve whatever problem the steam engine would have solved through sorcery directly?<br /> <br /> Ultimately, it seems to me that the only possible basis for major improvement in living standards at scale in Creation is magic, unless the gods or the Primordials antiqued Creation for some reason or put a bunch of fuel somewhere. Otherwise there's just not enough stored up energy to power an industrial society.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Coal certainly exists. I'd be surprised if oil didn't as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T04:51:45-08:00","id":1354550,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354536#post1354536\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Question. Is it possible to use elemental summoning and beckoning to power steam engines instead?</div> </div> </div> </div>I remember a more extreme version of this is how a rogue automaton-dragon came to be in 2e. I'd assume that apart from sorcerous skill, the biggest obstacle to doing this commercially is the sheer insult many elemental courts would perceive from mortal sorcerers even attempting it, to say nothing of Dragonblooded. Cue the returning Solars. This is a very specific case where a really good technologically advancement is dependant on the Solars for reasons that have nothing to do with their overall strength or particular Charms, and everything to do with the Mandate of Creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12360-guitar-longcat","name":"Guitar Longcat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T05:16:34-08:00","id":1354553,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sundance</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354535#post1354535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something is more important in Creation's technological state than the understanding of scientific principles, I think, is just the fact that (and I'm willing to be contradicted here if something in the canon is unknown to me) there's no reason for fossil fuels to exist. Not by natural means, anyway. Dead things putrefying into oil or coal is a process that takes millions of years, time Creation just hasn't had (the time scale of Primordial rule doesn't seem like it was *that* long). Without fuel, technical cleverness has a very, very hard time scaling. There just isn't practicality in making, say, a steam engine if the requisite heat to make a number of them work can only come from cutting down and burning acre upon acre of forest. Even if some brilliant savant thought through the engineering of such a device, it would never be more than a curiosity. I suppose that fuel could be made magically, but in that case why not just skip the step and solve whatever problem the steam engine would have solved through sorcery directly?<br /> <br /> Ultimately, it seems to me that the only possible basis for major improvement in living standards at scale in Creation is magic, unless the gods or the Primordials antiqued Creation for some reason or put a bunch of fuel somewhere. Otherwise there's just not enough stored up energy to power an industrial society.</div> </div> </div> </div>Things don't exist in Creation because of the same processes that exist on Earth, but because a bunch of mad titans made them. It doesn't take dinosaur carcasses undergoing the right chemical processes for millions of years for Creation to have oil, it takes one of the Primordials saying: &quot;guys, I know this sounds crazy, but hear me out - a liquid... that burns!&quot; while they were shaping the world whole cloth from the pure chaos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T05:39:46-08:00","id":1354558,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354399#post1354399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At least in 3e it's only animal life, but it's still yeah, pretty effing catostrophic. I tend to assume that the schtizobioms Creation has with creatures form Earth over 250M year history was probably like...more somehow back before then. Which gets weird when you get shit like I dunno, trilobytes in the ocena or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've pondered this before. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/65219-your-headcanon?p=1294697#post1294697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To reflect the fact that the Great Contagion killed more than the KT Extinction Event all First Age and Shogunate Lunar spirit shapes are wrong. The species of their spirit animals are still in the same family but reresent the huge amount of lost biodiversity so serious errors have been made.<br /> <br /> Raksi is a flying monkey.<br /> Leviathan is an orcapus.<br /> Ma-Ha-Suchi isn't a chimera, that's propagnada to hide the existence of The Goats Who Ate Men a scourge of the First Age now mercifully extinct.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#FFFFFF\">this is satire</span></div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T05:57:50-08:00","id":1354559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Why invest in alchemists to improve their technology of extracting cocaine from coca leaves when you can invest in an expedition to capture a dinosaur that pisses liquid cocaine? Why invest in your silver mining technology when a single artifact from a raided Solar tomb can net you a price that dwarfs the lifetime yield of several silver mines?</span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><br /> <br /> Why sail around the Cape of Good Hope or across the Atlantic to reach the Indies when you can just pay the fees of Venetians or Turks? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I heard a theory recently that the Roman Empire didn't undergo an Industrial Revolution of its own because slave labor effectively obviated the need for automation.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find there are several problems with that, such as Rome's slave population becoming something that stagnated and began dropping off when the expansion that was the primary source of renewal for it was significantly reduced (something that ultimately created an agrarian labour shortage that led to new arrangements with free people which served as the foundation of Medieval serfdom). As well as questions such as where a lot of the necessary raw materials came from. Or a lot of the capital. Actual industrialisation depended a lot on the first wave of imperialism, both for providing access to the network of materials that makes a lot of that stuff work and for having made the commercial sector lucrative enough to invest capital in risky ventures of invention and experimentation, as well as a class of people that can actually buy the increased output of products.<br /> <br /> As for the value of slave labour versus automation, we actually have a real world example in which those things were pitted directly against one another, and we all know how that turned out.<br /> <br /> Mind, we also kind of have parts of the world in which it's apparent that the two can go hand in hand, but that's a whole other tangent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really prefer taking from the larger spectrum of &quot;We can't have science&quot; (or rather, some characters can have science because this is sort of their paradigm) where there is no clear objective statement about &quot;The world works like this&quot;, and it can then be left to the interpretation and powers of the characters to try to understand what is and what is not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where you come down on how a given thing works does not obviate the validity of the scientific method if it's still a thing that can have rational models developed for how it works based on observation and experimentation.<br /> <br /> And not everything in the setting makes sense as something mystical. The functions of a counterweight catapult, for instance.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't think that the Realm has a direct capacity to &quot;suppress technology&quot; or even much of a concept of doing so, but think that the societies that arise from how it conducts itself in the world in its financial exploitation and frequent warfare (and the responding pressure from Lunar adversaries) are not conductive to many kinds of development. </span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T07:48:25-08:00","id":1354568,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354559#post1354559\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where you come down on how a given thing works does not obviate the validity of the scientific method if it's still a thing that can have rational models developed for how it works based on observation and experimentation.<br /> <br /> And not everything in the setting makes sense as something mystical. The functions of a counterweight catapult, for instance.</div> </div> </div> </div>My point is that our scientific method is based on the premise that there is physical causality, which has a lot of consequences (physical consequences have physical causes, similar experiences delivers the same result, etc ...). We do believe this physical causality exist, but we can not prove it not disprove it in our world, this is a meta physical premise. But building a setting where we say &quot;ok in this one we as creators of the setting know there is a physical causality and so science stuff&quot; makes me feel the same way I feel with the example I gave with the God premise. It's not a huge problem but I believe it tries to give not necessary answers to interesting but poorly-asked questions about how the world goes and make the setting lose a part of its value.<br /> <br /> That is why my first reaction to OP was : I really like your idea of we can't do science easily because you wanna do an experiment and suddenly bam a god falsifies everything, but I think there is a more deeper and interesting question in wondering if, on a disk created by the wills of Untold Beings, floating upon the madness of an unspeakable Chaos, there can be science as we know it (ie, is there the causality we believe adamantly there is in our world in this wonderful setting). This does not mean I don't want sciency things in the setting, nor that I think the world should not follow rules that <i>looks like </i>the causality we are used to, but that we should take the opportunity of the Exalted setting to ask different questions about our relation to science (what can we know when there are no definitive constants?), technological progress (can there be technological progress without science ?), education (what does it mean to learn non-universal truth?), and things like that.<br /> <br /> I hope I made my arguments clearer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T09:18:27-08:00","id":1354589,"title":"","content":"Kind of to follow up, something I think worth remembeirng. That being that engineering is <i>a science</i>. It is not, by itself, science. Even if there's something like a scientific method, whether that can be utilized by a large enough population of Creation to matter, or whether the isngihts found can be utilized for useufl engineering are separate matters which I find often get lost not in discussing fantasys ettings but also the real world at times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T09:49:59-08:00","id":1354594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354568#post1354568\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My point is that our scientific method is based on the premise that there is physical causality, which has a lot of consequences (physical consequences have physical causes, similar experiences delivers the same result, etc ...). We do believe this physical causality exist, but we can not prove it not disprove it in our world, this is a meta physical premise. But building a setting where we say &quot;ok in this one we as creators of the setting know there is a physical causality and so science stuff&quot; makes me feel the same way I feel with the example I gave with the God premise. It's not a huge problem but I believe it tries to give not necessary answers to interesting but poorly-asked questions about how the world goes and make the setting lose a part of its value.<br /> <br /> That is why my first reaction to OP was : I really like your idea of we can't do science easily because you wanna do an experiment and suddenly bam a god falsifies everything, but I think there is a more deeper and interesting question in wondering if, on a disk created by the wills of Untold Beings, floating upon the madness of an unspeakable Chaos, there can be science as we know it (ie, is there the causality we believe adamantly there is in our world in this wonderful setting). This does not mean I don't want sciency things in the setting, nor that I think the world should not follow rules that <i>looks like </i>the causality we are used to, but that we should take the opportunity of the Exalted setting to ask different questions about our relation to science (what can we know when there are no definitive constants?), technological progress (can there be technological progress without science ?), education (what does it mean to learn non-universal truth?), and things like that.<br /> <br /> I hope I made my arguments clearer.</div> </div> </div> </div>But Creation has physical causality, and we do know it for sure, because there's a huge-ass machine in Yu-Shan called the Loom of Fate that enforces it upon the world as its sole function.<br /> <br /> ...at least that's how it worked in 1e and 2e, but I don't think any of the 3e devs past or present would be willing to upturn the Word of Moran.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T10:30:31-08:00","id":1354604,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354594#post1354594\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But Creation has physical causality, and we do know it for sure, because there's a huge-ass machine in Yu-Shan called the Loom of Fate that enforces it upon the world as its sole function.<br /> <br /> ...at least that's how it worked in 1e and 2e, but I don't think any of the 3e devs past or present would be willing to upturn the Word of Moran.</div> </div> </div> </div>What I'm trying to argue is that you can't take a meta-physical problem and solve it with &quot;we have this magical stuff that solves it. Done.&quot;. Does life has meaning ? &quot;Yeah this Magical Tass says so thus we're good&quot; doesn't really sound like an interesting answer, so I'm trying to convince you (forum members) maybe there is a more complex reading of the topic at hand that can be done.<br /> <br /> I'm not trying to somehow prove anything about Creation or the setting itself that would be canon somewhere in the books, I'm trying to share on this forum the idea that seeing the science of Creation as &quot;ours with lasers and fireball&quot; is kind of missing the point of what is important about science and why we should question it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T10:45:07-08:00","id":1354609,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354604#post1354604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What I'm trying to argue is that you can't take a meta-physical problem and solve it with &quot;we have this magical stuff that solves it. Done.&quot;. Does life has meaning ? &quot;Yeah this Magical Tass says so thus we're good&quot; doesn't really sound like an interesting answer, so I'm trying to convince you (forum members) maybe there is a more complex reading of the topic at hand that can be done.<br /> <br /> I'm not trying to somehow prove anything about Creation or the setting itself that would be canon somewhere in the books, I'm trying to share on this forum the idea that seeing the science of Creation as &quot;ours with lasers and fireball&quot; is kind of missing the point of what is important about science and why we should question it.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I will point out that physical causality is only meta-physical in our world because we can't prove it. In Creation, it's literally the law of physics. And I will argue that that's what makes Creation more interesting than another setting where it's meta-physical, just like (to take your earlier example for a spin) it's interesting to play in settings where we know for a fact various forms of divinity exist, because this allows us to engage with something beyond our usual experience.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T11:20:20-08:00","id":1354622,"title":"","content":"I won't really try to argue much more, I think we just disagree on what we look for in the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T11:43:44-08:00","id":1354629,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epee102</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354546#post1354546\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Coal certainly exists. I'd be surprised if oil didn't as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oil is literally one of Autochthon's elements. Oil is probably rare in creation but it definitely exists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T11:51:49-08:00","id":1354631,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354629#post1354629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oil is literally one of Autochthon's elements. Oil is probably rare in creation but it definitely exists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering that both whales and olives exist in Creation I must concur that oil exists.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T12:33:19-08:00","id":1354649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354568#post1354568\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But building a setting where we say &quot;ok in this one we as creators of the setting know there is a physical causality and so science stuff&quot; makes me feel the same way I feel with the example I gave with the God premise. It's not a huge problem but I believe it tries to give not necessary answers to interesting but poorly-asked questions about how the world goes and make the setting lose a part of its value.</div> </div> </div> </div>Speaking as a person who does believe in God, I think one of those subjects has more immediate consequences than the other in any setting that doesn't strictly include God.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there is a more deeper and interesting question in wondering if [...] there can be science as we know it (ie, is there the causality we believe adamantly there is in our world in this wonderful setting). This does not mean I don't want sciency things in the setting, nor that I think the world should not follow rules that <i>looks like </i>the causality we are used to, but that we should take the opportunity of the Exalted setting to ask different questions about our relation to science (what can we know when there are no definitive constants?), technological progress (can there be technological progress without science ?), education (what does it mean to learn non-universal truth?), and things like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think those questions are perfectly capable of being asked in a world that still has it that, barring certain interventions or anomalies, objects can be redirected to fall to the ground.<br /> <br /> They're still questions that can be asked in reality, after all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T12:49:38-08:00","id":1354651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354594#post1354594\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But Creation has physical causality, and we do know it for sure, because there's a huge-ass machine in Yu-Shan called the Loom of Fate that enforces it upon the world as its sole function.<br /> <br /> ...at least that's how it worked in 1e and 2e, but I don't think any of the 3e devs past or present would be willing to upturn the Word of Moran.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like Jenna Moran as a writer and person a lot, but I'm sceptical of having this level of reverence for anybody and I think some of the things she suggested are worth giving another evaluation, especially if it keeps then consistent with other adjustments in the game's tone or focus.<br /> <br /> Also, the Loom of Fate was kind of glitchy. I'm not actually sure what Jenna wrote for that book besides the Charms (if anything), but suddenly imagining a connection between her computer science background and some ways in which the Loom was written.<br /> <br /> The matter of the world having been made deliberately by Primordials raises questions about what kinds of progenitors one wants them to be. Having extremely encompassing and highly rational intellects that could account for all contingencies and interactions might provide a means for them to rigourously design the world without them being to be nigh omnipotent, but might have issues for their portrayal elsewhere and keeping in the general time of the game.<br /> <br /> If immensely powerful and intelligent but still limited in the manner of a lot of mythic personalities, the subject of designing the world makes me think of an aphorism I heard a whole back: complex systems designed to be complex from the start tend to be riddled with flaws beyond the reckoning of the designer, whereas simple systems that accumulate complexity over time often manage to have more stable equilibrium.<br /> <br /> So to me it's a question of whether or not Primordials started with a full intelligent design that ends up having a lot of faults, or if they put together a seed of reality with some growth parameters that was then capable of growing under its own power.<br /> <br /> (Incidentally, crude oil comes from things that are far far older than the dinosaurs, as well as dying under more moist conditions)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T14:27:43-08:00","id":1354672,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354629#post1354629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oil is literally one of Autochthon's elements. Oil is probably rare in creation but it definitely exists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, think on it this way. Water in Creaiton probably can't be split into hydrogen and oxygen. Water in Creation is likely not H20. Similarly, while there are things we would consider oil and even oil-enough for things like lamps or cooking, I'm pretty skeptical Creation has the full range of hydrocarbons that we have in our own universe.<br /> <br /> (Also somehting-something, Kripke can go jump in the lake, don't @ me.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T14:36:34-08:00","id":1354673,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354649#post1354649\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think those questions are perfectly capable of being asked in a world that still has it that, barring certain interventions or anomalies, objects can be redirected to fall to the ground.<br /> <br /> They're still questions that can be asked in reality, after all.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think these questions really lose sense in the setting if we copy/paste our perception of our own science in the setting, but as said already I gave my opinion already I don't really plan on it.<br /> <br /> I didn't understand your first answer about God, I didn't plan to offend you just to give another example of things I think is a mistake to make clear statements in the setting because it gives too direct answers to these kind of things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-12T17:57:34-08:00","id":1354701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354672#post1354672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, think on it this way. Water in Creaiton probably can't be split into hydrogen and oxygen. Water in Creation is likely not H20. Similarly, while there are things we would consider oil and even oil-enough for things like lamps or cooking, I'm pretty skeptical Creation has the full range of hydrocarbons that we have in our own universe.<br /> <br /> (Also somehting-something, Kripke can go jump in the lake, don't @ me.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Given how it's used in Autochthonia I'm pretty sure it does come in the full range of hydrocarbons. Probably, different versions have impurities of other elements. Oil when reacted with fire transforms into smoke. Water when reacted with lightning transforms into air, given how flammable the products are maybe with fire impurities.<br /> <br /> Really, water probably has Hydrogen and Oxygen. It's just that in Creation the H2O molecule is more fundamental than the hydrogen and oxygen atoms. Maybe think of it how fundamental particles in quantum physics sometime spontaneously change into other fundamental particles.<br /> <br /> Edit: yeah after a little thought the alchemy of chemical processes makes a lot of sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":151,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","user":"Hark","date":". 12-12-2019, 08:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-13T04:05:40-08:00","id":1354745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354651#post1354651\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, the Loom of Fate was kind of glitchy. I'm not actually sure what Jenna wrote for that book besides the Charms (if anything), but suddenly imagining a connection between her computer science background and some ways in which the Loom was written.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's kind of what I'm getting at here. We call the way the Loom often malfunctions &quot;glitches&quot;, like the ones in computer code. One thing about glitches is that while unexpected, they are not random - the malfunction is specific in nature, it was caused by something in particular, and if that something is remedied, the glitch will go away. That kind of troubleshooting was always supposed to be one of the things Sidereals do, though it never got much support in the books. But that's beside the point. It being, even the way Creation's laws of physics sometimes <i>stop working</i> has its own internal logic that can be discerned, and it's a pretty rare feature in a fantasy setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-13T10:15:29-08:00","id":1354788,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354673#post1354673\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't understand your first answer about God</div> </div> </div> </div>An agnostic setting and an atheist setting are practically indistinguishable, where one that does or doesn't have firm ideas on whether or not reality can be assumed to have consistency can be expected to be quite different.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think is a mistake to make clear statements in the setting because it gives too direct answers to these kind of things.</div> </div> </div> </div>What even would be the context of making a declaration that this is a game in which God absolutely, positively does not exist? It feels like a very of thing to even bring up in the first place.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-13T10:18:31-08:00","id":1354789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354745#post1354745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's kind of what I'm getting at here. We call the way the Loom often malfunctions &amp;quot;glitches&amp;quot;, like the ones in computer code. One thing about glitches is that while unexpected, they are not random - the malfunction is specific in nature, it was caused by something in particular, and if that something is remedied, the glitch will go away. That kind of troubleshooting was always supposed to be one of the things Sidereals do, though it never got much support in the books. But that's beside the point. It being, even the way Creation's laws of physics sometimes <i>stop working</i> has its own internal logic that can be discerned, and it's a pretty rare feature in a fantasy setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, looking back on my post it strikes me as one of those instances of being contrarian without regard for my wider point.<br /> <br /> That point being the sense that some of the definition for the Loom of Fate might be worth revision.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-14T01:32:22-08:00","id":1354866,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354788#post1354788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">An agnostic setting and an atheist setting are practically indistinguishable, where one that does or doesn't have firm ideas on whether or not reality can be assumed to have consistency can be expected to be quite different.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the difference is that huge, or rather it's a ST choice to make the difference huge. I try to use this concept in my games and the biggest consequences are usually &quot;You don't know why it doesn't work there, maybe it was not meant to work more than once&quot;, then the player try to find some sort of explanation and another solution which offers variety in the game. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What even would be the context of making a declaration that this is a game in which God absolutely, positively does not exist? It feels like a very of thing to even bring up in the first place. </div> </div> </div>If you want to know, I feel the exact same way when I see a post about science in a RPG, hence my arguing here. I don't mean to be a jerk, I'm just defending a certain perspective on a topic I often see brought in discussions about games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-14T02:57:17-08:00","id":1354870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354788#post1354788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What even would be the context of making a declaration that this is a game in which God absolutely, positively does not exist? It feels like a very of thing to even bring up in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is the ultimate statement of Relative morality. Common to worlds where &quot;god is dead&quot; and might makes right, the absolute statement that there is no higher morality is used in fantasy worlds to contrast cultures and moralities in an edgy way. Especially settings which contain small-g gods. Especially settings where the protagonists wield god-like powers.<br /> <br /> I generally find agnoatic settings to be better written and less on-the-nose. But it's a valid stylistic choice to go full atheist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T14:24:28-08:00","id":1355946,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354672#post1354672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, think on it this way. Water in Creaiton probably can't be split into hydrogen and oxygen. Water in Creation is likely not H20. Similarly, while there are things we would consider oil and even oil-enough for things like lamps or cooking, I'm pretty skeptical Creation has the full range of hydrocarbons that we have in our own universe.<br /> <br /> (Also somehting-something, Kripke can go jump in the lake, don't @ me.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course not, they are pyroarbors formed from long chains of wood monads (Its natural state, look at how stringy plants are) with free phlosgistonic fire monads joined to the empty facets of the platonic solids.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354745#post1354745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's kind of what I'm getting at here. We call the way the Loom often malfunctions &quot;glitches&quot;, like the ones in computer code. One thing about glitches is that while unexpected, they are not random - the malfunction is specific in nature, it was caused by something in particular, and if that something is remedied, the glitch will go away. That kind of troubleshooting was always supposed to be one of the things Sidereals do, though it never got much support in the books. But that's beside the point. It being, even the way Creation's laws of physics sometimes <i>stop working</i> has its own internal logic that can be discerned, and it's a pretty rare feature in a fantasy setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that the operations of the loom are executed by sapient beings that explicitly have feelings and biases. I don't mean gods, but the Spiders, who can grow annoyed and change the local laws of physics to express their ire.<br /> <br /> In such a system, the laws of physics are closer to the (unwritten, instinctual) rules manual the pattern spiders follow which presumably someone could get access to or ask after.<br /> <br /> <br /> However, I think the mention of alchemy upthread is more useful for the vast majority of things. One of the fundamental differences between alchemy and chemistry is the importance of Will/consciousness. Transformation without are mirrored by transformations within. As such something like Willpower or Consciousness is a fundamental force or aspect of reality that is most certainly absent in standard RW scientific models.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T15:10:27-08:00","id":1355952,"title":"","content":"Or essence. So apparently you can spend essence to carry out alchemy. So is this... Creation ex nihilo?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T20:17:28-08:00","id":1355992,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1354870#post1354870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is the ultimate statement of Relative morality. Common to worlds where &quot;god is dead&quot; and might makes right, the absolute statement that there is no higher morality is used in fantasy worlds to contrast cultures and moralities in an edgy way.</div> </div> </div> </div></span></span></span><br /> </div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It can be, and often has been for better or worse, but the assertion that it must be is, if nothing else, kind of ignorant of the range or world views displayed by actual atheists.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Exthalion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1355946#post1355946\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, I think the mention of alchemy upthread is more useful for the vast majority of things. One of the fundamental differences between alchemy and chemistry is the importance of Will/consciousness. Transformation without are mirrored by transformations within. As such something like Willpower or Consciousness is a fundamental force or aspect of reality that is most certainly absent in standard RW scientific models.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure that as real life practices of alchemy went, the mirroring of transformations was concerned with the idea that manipulation of substances was essential for the refinement of the self, not the other way around. The transformation of base metals into gold wasn't a case of &quot;you have to really believe in it hard enough&quot;, it was the idea that if the correct, objectively functional formula for that transmutation could be discovered then engaging in the process would vastly elevate the spirit of the one doing so. As far as I'm aware, the point for dedicated alchemists was never about increasing stockpiles of gold as material wealth, it was about gold being pretty signifying it as a more rarefied mineral.<br /> <br /> And I suppose that one could see the circumstances in which people first witness chemical reactions while lacking even a basic underlying knowledge of how they occur or much sense for the practical applications and be amazed enough by it to think it was spiritually revelatory. And as time goes by, experimentation with materials and reactions would lead to alchemists gaining real insights into chemical properties that gets used to produce commercially viable substances at least as a side line. The spiritual stuff does eventually get crowded out by things like changing world views, the commercial aspect becoming increasingly significant, and the fact that it becomes hard for alchemists to ignore that the direction aligning with rational materialism does seem to end up yielding more engaging results. I mean, at some point those folks could not help but recognise that lead was not a less pure version of gold, but you mix it with the correct solutions and it yields the most interesting crystals.<br /> <br /> Turns out that the real way to convert lead into gold is to figure out ways of making marketable glass products from it.<br /> <br /> I say all of this as establishing part of the basis for why I'm often a bit dissatisfied with fictional properties that approach alchemy as a kind of all-encompassing magical chemistry where it's like... the spiritual exploration aspects are absent and it just goes with something like &quot;alchemy as the means of making whatever raw materials from what is at hand&quot; or &quot;alchemy as name for brewing outright potions&quot; in a society that still seems to have limited access to or application of these things.<br /> <br /> Now that being said, referring back to the world view underlying actual alchemy brings me to a point that another reason I could imagine rational materialism not having gained as much traction in Creation thus far is that it lends itself to having more directly empowered alternative world views and means of engaging with the world. I don't see that as meaning that rational materialism works less or is superseded or rendered unnecessary by magic, just an idea that systems pointing towards it never fully form for a variety of reasons, some of them being obstacles to the formations of those world views and social systems and some of them being people off on a number of other paths.<br /> <br /> As alchemy in the setting goes, I like it forming the basis of a sorcerous initiation (and even the idea that some of these things would be beneficial even for people that aren't even sorcerers), but have a few reservations about it having shown up as a Craft in the Hundred Devils write-up for bats of all places.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T22:08:12-08:00","id":1356000,"title":"","content":"I think of Essence akin to electricity in that it can do work (in the physics sense) without human input.<br /> <br /> In Exalted the electricity analogue is only available to some people - and they do great science with it like make warstriders.<br /> <br /> Science in real life was pretty slow before electricity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wrFXvyXnNjaFJ0XrHdIwce6rnG__0k9/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Sidereal Jumpstart</a> (3e fanwork Charmset)<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DQXwMXm8G1IEYLl-SmuFgKGugeZxQL63/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Cascades</a> (the above in flowchart form)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1103-tikor","name":"Tikor","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"826"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T22:25:31-08:00","id":1356003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1355992#post1355992\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm pretty sure that as real life practices of alchemy went, the mirroring of transformations was concerned with the idea that manipulation of substances was essential for the refinement of the self, not the other way around. The transformation of base metals into gold wasn't a case of &quot;you have to really believe in it hard enough&quot;, it was the idea that if the correct, objectively functional formula for that transmutation could be discovered then engaging in the process would vastly elevate the spirit of the one doing so. As far as I'm aware, the point for dedicated alchemists was never about increasing stockpiles of gold as material wealth, it was about gold being pretty signifying it as a more rarefied mineral.<br /> <br /> And I suppose that one could see the circumstances in which people first witness chemical reactions while lacking even a basic underlying knowledge of how they occur or much sense for the practical applications and be amazed enough by it to think it was spiritually revelatory. And as time goes by, experimentation with materials and reactions would lead to alchemists gaining real insights into chemical properties that gets used to produce commercially viable substances at least as a side line. The spiritual stuff does eventually get crowded out by things like changing world views, the commercial aspect becoming increasingly significant, and the fact that it becomes hard for alchemists to ignore that the direction aligning with rational materialism does seem to end up yielding more engaging results. I mean, at some point those folks could not help but recognise that lead was not a less pure version of gold, but you mix it with the correct solutions and it yields the most interesting crystals.<br /> <br /> Turns out that the real way to convert lead into gold is to figure out ways of making marketable glass products from it.<br /> <br /> I say all of this as establishing part of the basis for why I'm often a bit dissatisfied with fictional properties that approach alchemy as a kind of all-encompassing magical chemistry where it's like... the spiritual exploration aspects are absent and it just goes with something like &quot;alchemy as the means of making whatever raw materials from what is at hand&quot; or &quot;alchemy as name for brewing outright potions&quot; in a society that still seems to have limited access to or application of these things.<br /> <br /> Now that being said, referring back to the world view underlying actual alchemy brings me to a point that another reason I could imagine rational materialism not having gained as much traction in Creation thus far is that it lends itself to having more directly empowered alternative world views and means of engaging with the world. I don't see that as meaning that rational materialism works less or is superseded or rendered unnecessary by magic, just an idea that systems pointing towards it never fully form for a variety of reasons, some of them being obstacles to the formations of those world views and social systems and some of them being people off on a number of other paths.<br /> <br /> As alchemy in the setting goes, I like it forming the basis of a sorcerous initiation (and even the idea that some of these things would be beneficial even for people that aren't even sorcerers), but have a few reservations about it having shown up as a Craft in the Hundred Devils write-up for bats of all places.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a far more thought out response than my somewhat off the cuff post deserved, but I appreciate it none the less.<br /> <br /> In the most prosaic sense, I was referring to the fact that the mechanical resource of willpower must be invested in various sorcerous and thaumaturgical procedures, though an intense degree of focus and expenditure of mental energy might explain the same. <br /> There is probably a great deal of merit in the notion that other world views also offer viable paths to power or prosperity such that there may never have been a point where any was clearly superior, if indeed they were not outright syncretized in an attempt to capture multiple benefits.<br /> <br /> Tangentially, I am of the opinion that the collapse of civilization and the way that remnants were distributed may be another factor preventing the (re)emergence of a recognizable scientific tradition. While people have looked to reinvent the wheel, they generally don't. Solutions were already found to a great many of the material problems individuals and societies face in the past, and refined to a high degree by a civilization with time and resources to conduct large scale investment, research, and testing. Those with the natural talent, resources (including education), and supernatural to be best poised to make new discoveries and inventions will also be those most familiar with the old solutions and most able to at least approximate them.<br /> <br /> The graduates of the Heptogram, among the richest and most intelligent cohorts you are likely to find in Creation, are also the ones with the best access to copies of the demonologies of the First Age which will contain information on the ideal demon to solve most of their problems. (though there is room for discussion about the viability of reliance on demons)<br /> <br /> This is, I suppose, a subset of the lack of pressure to innovate. But I argue that situations of this type also... set the agenda? for intellection work. When innovation is required, there is an existing body of very useful techniques which will probably seem like the ideal seed from which to begin searching for a solution rather than starting wholly from scratch. If you need to pump water out of a mine, there are multiple solutions from summoned water elementals to bound pump demons to occult artifice which can do the job. If none of them exactly suit your particular mine, one of them seems like a more promising start than inventing the steam engine.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T06:15:19-08:00","id":1356072,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1355992#post1355992\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><br /> </div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It can be, and often has been for better or worse, but the assertion that it must be is, if nothing else, kind of ignorant of the range or world views displayed by actual atheists.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div>Sure. Plenty of athiests (anyone who subscribes to Rand or Rothbard) believe in objective morality.<br /> <br /> But in fiction there is a massive tonal change if god explicitly does not exist. Which isn't to say you can't tell these sorts of stories in worlds where god exists or may exist, but explicit atheism changes the subtext of the story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T12:01:35-08:00","id":1356138,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tikor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356000#post1356000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Science in real life was pretty slow before electricity.</div> </div> </div> </div>You know, at times like this I kind of wonder exactly what people mean by science, and what their baseline for how fast its investigation is.<br /> <br /> I mean, prior to the development of large scale commercially viable production of electricity, people effectively figured out (with mathematical precision) the principles guiding bodies in motion, transmissions of force, sound and light, various aspects of fluid dynamics, several qualities of thermodynamics, ways to precisely measure chemical reactions and quantities of substances. Like, before people were even able to directly verify the existence of the atom, they figured out how to effectively measure its mass. Like, they could take two different substances and tell you the exact mass in kilograms of each that would contain the exact same number of atoms (that's what the mole is).<br /> <br /> Absolute Zero is something that a person accurately theorized about based on collecting data on volume contractions correlated to reduction in temperature and drawing a graph.<br /> <br /> And, like, various parts of astronomy, although that's at least one area where Creation absolutely doesn't play by the rules. You can't really figure out the motion of the planets from an objective framework, because they really are moving randomly.<br /> <br /> I'd also find statements to the effect of &quot;it took thousands of years&quot; are technically accurate but not quite assigning the blame correctly. Because again, a lot of this stuff could only effectively make progress during periods of social stability and prosperity.<br /> <br /> Still, I don't know, sometimes I feel as though people who talk a lot about science don't quite know a whole lot about its actual history or composition. At the very least, the they don't often seem to talk about specific things. Some of that makes me think it has less to do with the rate of scientific development and more with Newtonian physics, determining the mass of the mole and extrapolating absolute zero from graph paper lacks the sexiness of rocket launches and the electronic telegraph to most people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T13:12:48-08:00","id":1356147,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I think it was around steam power that tech really got moving.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T14:10:05-08:00","id":1356161,"title":"","content":"Science <i>isn't</i> stagnant in Creation. The issue is:<br /> <br /> 1. During the Solar Deliberative era, a lot of technology and scientific understanding was derived from the Solars, since they had the best crafters.<br /> <br /> 2. During the Shogunate, that Solar-centric technology and understanding declined, and while the Dragon-Blooded made new things to replace it, the Sidereals and Lunars were sometimes actively sabotaging them (for different reasons - the Sidereals wanted them to be easier to manage and less likely to blow themselves up, the Lunars just hated their guts.) Keep in mind that this was about 750 years, so you could still have seen eg. the level of real-world technological advancement from 500 BC to 250 AD or something. It also wasn't as noticeable because a lot of it was replacing stuff they previously had rather than totally new advancements. This handwavy sense of discrete obvious advancement along our history is absurdly silly and oversimplified, but it gets the idea across.<br /> <br /> 3. The Contagion hit and everyone was fucked. 90% of everything died. Creation is literally post-apocalyptic. This isn't, like, the black death in the real world - society flat-out collapsed and a ton of stuff was lost.<br /> <br /> 4. It's been another 750 years since then. Again, 750 years from the total collapse of civilization isn't long - going by the stupid oversimplification above, it *might* reach 1000 AD if we ignore the end of the world in between.<br /> <br /> 5. That said, there is some genuine resistance to advancement. There are gods and exalted with reasons to take actions that prevent technological advancement, directly or indirectly. Look at what happened in real-world China around 1500 AD for an example, when the leaders of China increasingly turned it inwards because they feared (correctly) that trade was empowering people who could challenge their authority. There's no one central anti-technology group among the DBs (although there <i>may</i> be some of this among the Sidereals and Lunars), but there's a lot of DBs who will crush upstart mortal endeavors or redirect resources in nepotistic ways that make it hard for new ideas to thrive.<br /> <br /> 6. Technology isn't, like, some sort of magic unified tech tree that everyone progresses along in the same way. But the main issue is that the sort of &quot;advancement&quot; you're thinking of requires that ideas be preserved and disseminated. Creation has mostly been bad at that and has had catastrophes interfering with it for the reasons listed above. Individual areas have their own technology and advancement, but something like an Industrial Revolution can't happen without a lot more stable exchange and preservation of ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 12-20-2019, 05:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T17:05:53-08:00","id":1356198,"title":"","content":"It's hard to cleanly preserve and transmit the ideas of entire generations, to build a foundation for the next generation to advance on, if one's society doesn't consider education valuable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T21:35:39-08:00","id":1356234,"title":"","content":"One thing is the difference between accumulating technical innovations, and actual science.<br /> <br /> The East Asian, Indian, and Muslim civilization-complexes did accumulate technical innovations, but they never had a full Scientific Revolution. That only happened in Western Christian civilization. Why?<br /> <br /> Well, one of the obvious candidates is that Western Christianity incorporated Aristotle into standard theology in the 13th Century under Aquinas, while the other major civilizations did not (either explicitly rejecting him like the Muslim theologians, or never even having him come to enough attention to consider in India and East Asia). Since Aristotle had engaged in rational inquiry into natural phenomena, the incorporation meant there was a certain derived respectability to doing the same which didn't happen in societies where religion-endorsed philosophy did not include investigation into natural phenomena.<br /> <br /> Applying that to Creation, with gods and elementals directly involved in physical phenomena, it is not just somewhat harder to investigate; it is likely actively discouraged for mortals to poke their noses in. While that doesn't apply to the Dragon-Blooded, they're still going to be part of an overall culture that discourages that. Throw in how much knowledge was lost with the fall of the First Age and then with the Great Contagion, and the budding investigators among the Dragon-Blooded are probably going to divert themselves more into trying to recover old knowledge than making new.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":572,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","user":"Verzio","date":". 12-21-2019, 07:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T00:39:00-08:00","id":1356255,"title":"","content":"Fun history factoid on Aristotle actually is Europe kind of...<i>didn't</i> pay attention to Aristotle a lot. They loved sylogistic logic but it's kind of notable that folks just kind of took stuff he said on biology just as like...solved and taken for granted. And later authors like Galan (anatomy/medicne) and Theophrastus (botany) were kind of assumed for a while. Very little like, zoology or botany as we recognize it was like...published until the late 1300s. Even though folks had had Aristotle and Theophrastus in some form already for a while.<br /> <br /> Hell, that we even have Aristotle is kind of weird. After he went back to Macedon, his works were apparently lost <i>in antiquity </i>for like a century. Some later Greeks found him and it was them and the Romans who kept him in circulation. He had whole swaths apparently of Plato-style dailectics but all we have today is his professoral lecture notes.<br /> <br /> Kind of worth noting how fragile knowledge and ideas can be. Even granting Aristotlelian was kind of important for the West, that Aristotle himself was nearly lost a couple times along hte way is kind of an example advancement being as much a matter of random dumb luck as anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T04:36:51-08:00","id":1356274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356234#post1356234\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since Aristotle had engaged in rational inquiry into natural phenomena, the incorporation meant there was a certain derived respectability to doing the same which didn't happen in societies where religion-endorsed philosophy did not include investigation into natural phenomena.</div> </div> </div> </div>Encouragement for investigation of natural phenomena has often been interpreted as being written right into the Qu'ran, and might be associated with the fact that the Islamic world has had, for instance, sophisticated treatises on the science of optics.<br /> <br /> The science of optics being making and recording observations on the manner in which light is transmitted through materials to both make reasonable models for prediction and form a basis to speculate on what the fundamental nature of light is.<br /> <br /> But again, not rocket science, so I guess the question of how it's investigated in Creation just kind of gets overlooked.<br /> <br /> A reason that peer review is very important to modern scientific inquiry and it's useful for general economics combined with deliberate state endeavours to funnel larger numbers of people than ever before into scientific education is that the most accurate models are dependent on <i>huge</i> amounts of raw experimental data that can be collated to produce ever more precisely calibrated curves. It's the reason why a properly managed drug trial takes years before the thing is permitted on the open market; any given singular trial can only involve a few dozen, maybe a few hundred people, and you want to ensure as much uniformity in the variables as possible both to be sure that the thing genuinely works and all those people didn't show improvements as the result of a fluke, while both spreading it across as large a sample size as you can get to see how it interacts with several variables (to find out if certain specific interactions make it dangerous).<br /> <br /> Scientific inquiry is always going to be slow when all of the experiments have to be conducted by lone eccentrics and their assistants, and all of the data needs to be stored on paper, and all the calculations done by hand, and the degree to which the educated can consult with one another is limited. I was told that the graph from which absolute zero was derived was massive, took up the floor space of a large room; that may have been an exaggeration, but it has a ring of truth from considering how many experimental observations of temperature and volume would have been necessary to form a sufficiently accurate curve, and the time that would have taken even before the graph is drawn out.<br /> <br /> I don't know, what god is terribly concerned with you making observations that can be used to form precise models of how pressure works?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T05:15:47-08:00","id":1356276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356255#post1356255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even granting Aristotlelian was kind of important for the West, that Aristotle himself was nearly lost a couple times along hte way is kind of an example advancement being as much a matter of random dumb luck as anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. He was really a quite secondary philosopher in terms of influence from when he taught until the Arabs conquered half the Roman Empire and got their hands on him, ever in the shade of Plato. And of course Plato taught that the world was a shadow of reality, and a proper philosopher looked at reality directly through his mind rather than through the shadows it cast in matter. Under that sort of paradigm, proto-science is low prestige.<br /> <br /> It's really an obvious matter of historical contingency that, first, a useful quantity of Aristotelian philosophy still managed to survive, and second, the efforts by some Catholic theologians to ban &quot;the new Aristotle&quot; (after the Arabs transmitted it to the Latin West) in favor of maintaining neo-Platonist dominance didn't succeed. if it had been theologically crushed like it was in the Arab world by <i>The Incoherence of the Philosophers</i>, what study of &quot;natural philosophy&quot; would there have been anywhere on Earth? Technology would have continued to accumulate, and mathematics as an intellectual toy, and maybe that <i>eventually</i> would create the proper takeoff by its own accumulation, but, well, all we know is that it <i>didn't</i> take off except in the one civilization that called Aristotle &quot;The Master of Those Who Know&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T06:02:15-08:00","id":1356281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356274#post1356274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Encouragement for investigation of natural phenomena has often been interpreted as being written right into the Qu'ran, and might be associated with the fact that the Islamic world has had, for instance, sophisticated treatises on the science of optics.</div> </div> </div> </div>And resulted in a Scientific Revolution in the Muslim world, right?<br /> <br /> Oh, wait, no, it didn't. Islam had a true Golden Age of natural philosophy under the influence of Greek philosophy, but it died. Because the theologian Al-Ghazali (honored by the title &quot;The Proof of Islam&quot;, the most important theologian in Sunni Muslim history other than Muhammad himself) said to believe in the operation of the world by natural law rather than by direct divine miracle was heresy. And thus even if some bits of the Qu'ran could be interpreted as encouraging investigation into nature, it didn't matter, because it was socially difficult to try to investigate laws of nature in a society where people were predisposed to see that as evidence of heresy.<br /> <br /> So the great intellectual heirs of Ibn al-Haytham (the author of the <i>Book of Optics</i> who died about twenty years before Al-Ghazali was born) accordingly were natural philosophers in medieval Catholic universities, not Muslims, whatever the Qu'ran might be argued to say. Because what mattered was the attitude of the society.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356274#post1356274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I don't know, what god is terribly concerned with you making observations that can be used to form precise models of how pressure works?</div> </div> </div> </div>You don't need a <i>god</i> concerned about that. You need a social environment that isn't crushing <i>people</i> interested in that, in the nascent period where 1) it's actually possible for small numbers of investigators to actually learn important things, and 2) learning those things isn't translating into major technological breakthroughs yet, so it isn't obviously of practical value. When 1 accumulates to the point you get 2, <i>then</i> you get the prestige to actually get active support. But that seems, based on the historical models we have, to only come after natural philosophy is tolerated as a respectable sub-field of philosophy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T06:20:29-08:00","id":1356285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356281#post1356281\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And resulted in a Scientific Revolution in the Muslim world, right?<br /> <br /> Oh, wait, no, it didn't.</div> </div> </div> </div>My point is to not reduce things down to singular pivots and to withhold contempt.<br /> <br /> For any given part of the world, it's a spectrum across the length of history, not a binary on whether or not their entire philosophical tradition came down to yes or no.<br /> <br /> Along certain axes, the Aristotelian tradition can be seen as a hindrance as much as help when it encourages discovery through inductive reasoning while disdaining practical experimentation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You need a social environment that isn't crushing <i>people</i> interested in that, in the nascent period where 1) it's actually possible for small numbers of investigators to actually learn important things, and 2) learning those things isn't translating into major technological breakthroughs yet, so it isn't obviously of practical value. When 1 accumulates to the point you get 2, <i>then</i> you get the prestige to actually get active support. But that seems, based on the historical models we have, to only come after natural philosophy is tolerated as a respectable sub-field of philosophy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly, that is a complex web of sensitive influences.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I think the nuances of the philosophical and intellectual history of Islam are important to maintain in any discussion like this, because the reductive narrative of Muslims being superstitious and ignorant contributes to rhetoric that has real negative consequences in modern societies with a strong inclination to Islamophobia.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 12-21-2019, 09:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T06:45:04-08:00","id":1356287,"title":"","content":"These intellectual history -&gt; science ideas, tend to seem to postulate that ideas of scientific methods were either really uncommon, or common but commonly suppressed / not transmitted. So taking Verzio's example, you need Aristotle (unusual) and then for his ideas not to be suppressed (again unusual).<br /> <br /> (This specific one seems in some ways like the flipside of ideas, in a way, that early Medieval Europe and European Christianity was uniquely suppressive of scientific ideas?)<br /> <br /> But I think it's really hard to know through if this was the case without very thick knowledge of what people thought that even specialists would find challenging. This tends to be why I would tend to go more materialist and estimate that these ideas probably were not that unusual, but that the instruments and tools (printing press, optics, mathematical notation) may have come together more in Europe in a way that allowed the ideas to be useful. These instruments and tools (used to test and communicate) themselves not necessarily coming from science, but from experience based learning. So this is a much more materialist idea that tends to stress a more general ability to produce tools and mechanisms across the society that then made scientific ideas, a minority in any society, more useful and practical to apply.<br /> <br /> If we're looking for intellectual currents, I would guess you may also see more of the influence of a mechanistic view of the cosmos in Europe, connecting with a generally greater use of mechanical technology over the late Middle Ages (mills, clocks, etc.) to early modern period than what we would see elsewhere in Eurasia (certainly from what I know than in the world of Islam and Near East, and also China and India), which in turn is possibly driven by different subsistence ecology and economies and state structures, etc.<br /> <br /> Ultimately though it is very difficult to know what is correct.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T06:58:50-08:00","id":1356291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356285#post1356285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the reductive narrative of Muslims being superstitious and ignorant </div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, even implying that this is the extent of what has been said by another is my own instance of being reductive, and I can't in good conscience or intellectual honesty let it pass without my apology for becoming heated enough to hint at putting words in somebody's mouth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T07:06:34-08:00","id":1356293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356287#post1356287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If we're looking for intellectual currents, I would guess you may also see more of the influence of a mechanistic view of the cosmos in Europe, connecting with a generally greater use of mechanical technology over the late Middle Ages (mills, clocks, etc.) to early modern period than what we would see elsewhere in Eurasia (certainly from what I know than in the world of Islam and Near East, and also China and India), which in turn is possibly driven by different subsistence ecology and economies and state structures, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the early modern period is also one in which those particular forms of rationalism, materialism and secular humanism also gain traction for reasons including being a particular reaction to growing issues that people had with the Catholic Church, which arise from an intersection of beliefs, practices and the authority and property that the Church held that holds many distinctions from other religious bodies across history.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T09:30:20-08:00","id":1356310,"title":"","content":"It depends in what sense you mean that. The most famous thinker who is really having a mechanistic view of metaphysics and reality is probably Descartes right? As we all know from our RE lessons - Watchmaker analogy. And he styled himself as a devout Catholic and thought this was in defense of Catholicism.<br /> <br /> Now, that same Catholic Church may not have agreed that it was, but I don't think you can get the sense that the driver for these mechanistic analogies was dissatisfaction with or challenge towards the Established Church, as they seem to arise and become popular about the same time and to a similar degree in thinkers that perceived themselves as belonging to the Church or unassociated to it (e.g. Newton as essentially non-Trinitarian heretic).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T11:14:45-08:00","id":1356333,"title":"","content":"The reason why European civilization suddenly accelerated rapidly between 1500 and ~1900 when others in comparable positions did not is complex; historians call it the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Divergence\" target=\"_blank\">Great Divergence</a>, and you can read some commonly-accepted explanations there. (Spoiler: &quot;Aristotle was really smart&quot; is not one of them, no.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-21T18:23:07-08:00","id":1356409,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356291#post1356291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm, even implying that this is the extent of what has been said by another is my own instance of being reductive, and I can't in good conscience or intellectual honesty let it pass without my apology for becoming heated enough to hint at putting words in somebody's mouth.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay. To make it absolutely clear, I don't think there was anything <i>inherent</i> to Christianity or Islam that explains why Aristotle became the basis of learning in Catholic Europe. The fact that Aristotle was known and ignored in all the rest of the Christian world (all of it pre-Aquinas, and always in the Byzantine, Russian, and Ethiopian areas), that he wasn't initially ignored in the Muslim world, and the virtual coin flip in the case of the University of Paris as to whether the &quot;New Aristotle&quot; would be banned make it clear there's nothing about the religion itself that drove it. It's easy to imagine a world where Ibn Rushd (Averroes) won the argument in the Muslim world and Aquinas lost in the Catholic, and the dominant Muslim civilization talks about how Christians were held back because their religion fosters superstition and ignorance. It didn't happen that way, but why it didn't is a matter of social currents too subtle for examination of history to reach definitive conclusions.<br /> <br /> Now, is it reductive to point specifically to a single point cause with the Aristotle-baptized-by-Aquinas? Well, maybe. But the thing is, when X is universal in all cases <i>except one</i>, it is usually because of what was different in that one case, rather than a bunch of single accidents in all the other cases. (Not always, but . . .)<br /> <br /> So when people, like in Aquilion's link, start dragging out &quot;causes&quot; like Confucianism (which of course didn't apply in the Muslim or Indian civilization-complexes), I think they're nuts. Same with the people blaming India's caste system, which had so many parallels in Christian Europe (nobles/townsmen/serfs/Cagots-or-nightsoil-workers-or . . .) that our theoretical advanced Muslim world could blame it with more justice than we could. Or blaming political unity, which doesn't characterize the Muslim world much more accurately than it did in Europe (Persians versus Ottomans, weakness of Ottoman rule in Egypt and North Africa compared with weakness of Hapsburg rule in the HRE, the Ottomans clearly being in competition with the same Europeans anyway, etc.), and what unity was there in India, and . . .<br /> <br /> My core is, by a contingent accident (Aristotle becoming the basis of its theology), western Europe wound up with &quot;natural philosophy&quot; as field of (consistently) respectable intellectual inquiry, while nobody else did. And then it's where natural philosophy evolved into science, an evolution which would seem to require that you have a vigorous tradition of natural philosophy. However fertile a farm is, it won't grow wheat unless there's wheat seed in it; a less-fertile field that's been sewn with wheat will grow more than the rich-but-fallow one. And the availability of wheat seed has little or nothing to do with the quality of the soil.<br /> <br /> Whether <i>science</i> then drove the material Great Divergence is then its own question, but I note that the &quot;late date&quot; view (which I favor) puts it downstream from the Scientific Revolution.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":572,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","user":"Verzio","date":". 12-21-2019, 09:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-22T05:40:20-08:00","id":1356469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356333#post1356333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason why European civilization suddenly accelerated rapidly between 1500 and ~1900 when others in comparable positions did not is complex; historians call it the <a href=\"http://&quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Divergence&quot;]Great</a> Divergence[/URL], and you can read some commonly-accepted explanations there. (Spoiler: &quot;Aristotle was really smart&quot; is not one of them, no.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure you've fully parsed your own link.<br /> <br /> Aristotle's just war ethics, virtue theory, and property theories all seem to be definitive factors according to that wikipedia link.<br /> <br /> And that article makes it look pretty complex. But that's by-the-by.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 12-22-2019, 08:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-22T06:20:52-08:00","id":1356473,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356469#post1356469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And that article makes it look pretty complex.</div> </div> </div> </div>History generally is.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-02T08:47:27-08:00","id":1358426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1356138#post1356138\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know, at times like this I kind of wonder exactly what people mean by science, and what their baseline for how fast its investigation is.</div> </div> </div> </div>The discovery of truth in its predictive form. The ability to tell the future through observing the present and past. An understanding of our world that matches the course of future events. As an example, both the necessary knowledge to know the chemical reaction that will drive a piston, as well the materials science to contain and direct that force for locomotion. The ability to coax agriculture into yields our ancestors would have considered magical. And how shit-slow any of that type of thing was when there were fewer of us, and most scratched subsistence from the earth all day.<br /> <br /> I was being glib talking about electricity. To risk being boringly serious and earthbound, mostly it was the rare eccentric who made scientific discoveries in the pre-modern world. Today, being a scientist is a respected profession staffed by more people today<i> than there were even people at all</i> in most of human history (setting an arbitrary flag down at, say, 2 million years ago). Having achieved a better ratio of people discovering things to things to be discovered, we're making more progress per unit time (assuming a constant progress per unit person-time).<br /> <br /> Creation leans hard into the 'rare eccentric moving the world' theme. It makes sense that its progress would be slow, waiting on these geniuses to arrive instead of harnessing institutional power to the cause of (mortal) discovery. I understand the secrets of Essence and Charms are well plumbed (martial arts dojos, sorcerous schools, etc.), but they are only accessible to the few.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>Things the ancients discovered, generally. I, Tikor, paraphrase from</i><i> Isator Levi</i><i>.</i> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, they discovered some cool stuff. That didn't make the 1310's much different from the 1320's, besides which petty tyrant controlled what patch of dirt. We're arguing rate here, not distance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wrFXvyXnNjaFJ0XrHdIwce6rnG__0k9/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Sidereal Jumpstart</a> (3e fanwork Charmset)<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DQXwMXm8G1IEYLl-SmuFgKGugeZxQL63/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Cascades</a> (the above in flowchart form)","edited":{"userid":1103,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1103-tikor","user":"Tikor","date":". 01-02-2020, 11:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1103-tikor","name":"Tikor","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"826"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-02T13:22:54-08:00","id":1358475,"title":"","content":"Extended Nitpic: There were no humans 2 million years ago. Anatomically modern humans emerged some 350k years ago, and they certainly weren't building cities. Agriculture is some 12k years old, and that is when the population &quot;boom&quot; started.<br /> <br /> By your definition of telling the future through observing the present and past, that goes back to the stone age, some 10,000 years with calendars that predicted the changing seasons based on sun and star patterns. 6k years ago in Egypt they noted that the rise of Sirius indicated the season of inundation was coming and built their calendar around it. Around 5k years ago nilometers were invented that used the level of the floodwater to predict the output of the harvest, (with marks for what levels would bring good years, famine, damaging floods, etc.)<br /> <br /> For goodness sakes, Heroditus and Aristotle were looking for causal relationships in history, geography, and nature. They were very frequently wrong and working from flawed methods, but they were trying.<br /> <br /> The idea that truth is predictive is extremely old.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-02T13:35:37-08:00","id":1358476,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Exthalion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1358475#post1358475\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By your definition of telling the future through observing the present and past, that goes back to the stone age, some 10,000 years with calendars that predicted the changing seasons based on sun and star patterns. 6k years ago in Egypt they noted that the rise of Sirius indicated the season of inundation was coming and built their calendar around it. Around 5k years ago nilometers were invented that used the level of the floodwater to predict the output of the harvest, (with marks for what levels would bring good years, famine, damaging floods, etc.)<br /> <br /> For goodness sakes, Heroditus and Aristotle were looking for causal relationships in history, geography, and nature. They were very frequently wrong and working from flawed methods, but they were trying.<br /> <br /> The idea that truth is predictive is extremely old.</div> </div> </div> </div>Counterpoint:<br /> <br /> The Egyptians also thought the Nile was flooded by the god Hapi, and the nilometers were monitored by his priests who performed mystic rituals to appease him.<br /> <br /> When you stop basing your truth on the observed evidence and start basing it on myth... that's... not science.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-02T17:28:07-08:00","id":1358504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1358476#post1358476\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Counterpoint:<br /> <br /> The Egyptians also thought the Nile was flooded by the god Hapi, and the nilometers were monitored by his priests who performed mystic rituals to appease him.<br /> <br /> When you stop basing your truth on the observed evidence and start basing it on myth... that's... not science.</div> </div> </div> </div>So there were no doctors in the world until chemists isolated the active ingredient in remedies? The four humors, alchemy, the luminiferous ether, all of them are just as unreal as Hapi and people did things based on them that were just as efficacious as those mystic rituals.<br /> <br /> When your standard for science is &quot;was not later replaced with a better explanation&quot; then nothing we now do is science in the long run.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T01:00:12-08:00","id":1358566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Exthalion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1358504#post1358504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So there were no doctors in the world until chemists isolated the active ingredient in remedies? The four humors, alchemy, the luminiferous ether, all of them are just as unreal as Hapi and people did things based on them that were just as efficacious as those mystic rituals.<br /> <br /> When your standard for science is &quot;was not later replaced with a better explanation&quot; then nothing we now do is science in the long run.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think what they were doing was science as defined by modern standards (Galileo, Descartes, Newton, etc ...) because they seemed to lack 2 major elements : observable reality and empirical methodology (I don't know them specifically I know Greeks a bit better but I believe Egyptians have many elements in common with Greeks). This does not mean they were stupid, had no rationality or had no common points with the scientific paradigm that emerged later around The Renaissance, but I think there is a difference in nature in their methodology that is sufficient to say their practice differs greatly with the science as we conceived it later.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T05:37:37-08:00","id":1358600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Exthalion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1358504#post1358504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So there were no doctors in the world until chemists isolated the active ingredient in remedies? The four humors, alchemy, the luminiferous ether, all of them are just as unreal as Hapi and people did things based on them that were just as efficacious as those mystic rituals.<br /> <br /> When your standard for science is &quot;was not later replaced with a better explanation&quot; then nothing we now do is science in the long run.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it's based on observation and testing, but is wrong, then it's science. Newton thinking gravity worked instantaneously is a mistake, but it's science.<br /> <br /> Saying &quot;god keeps us pinned to the world with his giant invisible hand&quot; is not based on observation and testing -- it's not science.<br /> <br /> That ancient cultures relied on methods other than observation and testing to create predictable models is not a value judgement. But my belief that Ahura Mazda caused the Big Bang is not a scientific belief. It's not something testable based on observation.<br /> <br /> So no, the four humors aren't science. Or rather, they were science right up to the point where no-one noticed that bleeding people because they had a fever caused them to die, at which point bleeding people anyway became a quack mystic belief which was continued for centuries based on tradition -- not science.<br /> <br /> Science can be (and nearly universally is) wrong. But it has to be something testable based on observation. &quot;Because we've always done it that way&quot; or &quot;Because god said so&quot; isn't science (even when it is correct).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chausse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1354207-thoughts-on-why-science-is-stagnant-in-creation?p=1358566#post1358566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think what they were doing was science as defined by modern standards (Galileo, Descartes, Newton, etc ...) because they seemed to lack 2 major elements : observable reality and empirical methodology (I don't know them specifically I know Greeks a bit better but I believe Egyptians have many elements in common with Greeks). This does not mean they were stupid, had no rationality or had no common points with the scientific paradigm that emerged later around The Renaissance, but I think there is a difference in nature in their methodology that is sufficient to say their practice differs greatly with the science as we conceived it later.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly this.<br /> <br /> It's possible to discover truths without employing the scientific method.<br /> <br /> Which, I admit, is moving the goal posts from Tikor's initial definition of &quot;predictive truth&quot;. But I strongly feel that &quot;science&quot; is something that has a definition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 01-03-2020, 12:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T09:52:51-08:00","id":1358636,"title":"","content":"This seems to have veered into semantics and creating of post facto definitions intended to create a demarcating line at a desired historical point by using historical developments as the criteria.<br /> <br /> Chausse's definition leaves both of the important terms undefined. Observable reality and empirical methodology? Assuming the ancients weren't writing down their dreams they were observing the same reality as we are, and empirical literally means from the senses which is one of the criticisms of figures like Aristotle, that they based too much on what is observable to unaided human senses.<br /> <br /> So as to avoid the appearance of attacking the definition because I do not wish to appear wrong, Carl Popper argued that the defining characteristic of a science is generating falsifiable predictions. If falsifying evidence is found, something must change be it revising the theory or accepting an alternative.<br /> <br /> Newton made falsifiable predictions about the motions of the planets that were proven to be incorrect so Relativity was adopted.<br /> <br /> Aristotle made falsifiable predictions about moving bodies that were disproved by Gallileo. (who was disproved by Newton in turn)<br /> <br /> Both Newton and Aristotle created their predictions in the same way. They gathered sensory data using the best equipment available at the time (naked eye vs telescope) and proposed a framework to unify and explain those observations.<br /> <br /> Thus by Popper's definition, Aristotle was doing science. It was not, by modern standards, very good science because he lacked sophisticated instruments or compiled data which didn't exist at the time and he did not perform experiments under controlled conditions to obtain purer data. However, within his framework, the entire world was what amounted to a laboratory as everyday experience generally conformed to his predictions within the observational margin of error for most people.<br /> <br /> NOW! Popper's definition is not the only definition. Other philosophers of science have taken issue with it. Chausse proposed a different one. Saying the Ancients were doing science does not mean they were doing it better than today or that all of what they were doing was science. The priests of Hapi could be doing science when they constructed the nilometer and religion when they prayed for a good harvest. But the nilometer itself is a result of falsifiable predictions. &quot;If the flood of the Nile falls reaches of a hight between X and Y (as measured by this device) at time T then there will be a good harvest. Less than X and there will be famine. Greater than Y and there will be damaging floods.&quot; And they kept records and (very occasionally) revised the X and Y lines. The ancillary prayer is not a bad apple that poisons the whole barrel. It had better not be, or else Newton's alchemy would be quite damning.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1144,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","user":"Exthalion","date":". 01-03-2020, 12:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T10:30:10-08:00","id":1358644,"title":"","content":"One of Aristotle's demonstration of the sphericity of the world was based on a multi-layered arguments : The composition of the world in multiple elements and the equality in properties of a perfect being. To my (limited) knowledge, these arguments were, at the time, not based on empirical observation of their world through repeatable and falsifiable experience but a product of their theology and philosophy. This does not mean that Aristotle and his Egyptians peers didn't spend a lot of time thinking masterfully about these subjects nor that they did zero experience about their surroundings, but rather that their paradigms was very different of the one that came after them, even if they strongly influenced and inspired what would become science at the end of the middle-age. So they are father of science, without a doubt, but I think there are paradigm gaps between their method and what we accept today as the scientific method.<br /> <br /> I would also argue that Popper's falsifiability is not a <i>sufficient </i>property but only a <i>necessary</i> property of the scientific process, the same way than the criteria I gave are not sufficient but only necessary to establish a scientific process. I would also add, but I'm not an expert on the subject, that Popper's definition and the criteria I gave may be redundant to some extent : for example an empirical methodology usually induces repeatable experience which is a cornerstone of falsifiability (the ability to produce experiences that disprove the theory).<br /> <br /> \u200bI must admit this is going pretty far and I am by no mean an expert on scientific epistemology so I'm open to having an expert on this topic contradict me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}